WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:06.020
All right.

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:07.960
Let's get started.

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:11.300
So last time, Tatsu
talked about scaling laws,

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:14.240
and we're going to take
a little break from that

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:17.840
and talk about an inference.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:21.760
So the problem of
inference is very simple.

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:24.820
You've trained a model,
and you're given a prompt,

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:27.480
and you want to produce
the response usually

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:31.600
as accurately and as
quickly as you can.

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:35.820
So inference turns out
to be only one lecture,

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:39.640
but it's actually of
growing importance.

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:42.680
And it shows up in many places.

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:44.580
So clearly, once
you've trained a model,

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:45.980
you don't just sit there.

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:48.200
And if you're a
researcher, you put

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:50.940
a plot in your paper of
here's how my model works,

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:54.300
but for everyone else, you
actually want to use this model.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:56.120
So what does use look like?

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:59.830
It could be chatting with
an AI assistant or chatbot,

00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:02.670
it could be doing
code completion.

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:04.950
These days, agents
are very popular,

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:08.030
and that requires inference.

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:12.430
Batch data processing as well.

00:01:12.430 --> 00:01:15.790
It's also used for evaluation.

00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:19.670
For evaluation that requires
generation in particular.

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:21.565
And it's also used
inside training.

00:01:21.565 --> 00:01:23.190
If you're doing
reinforcement learning,

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:25.750
you need to have a model,
you generate rollouts,

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:27.750
and you score them, and
you update the weights

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:28.950
appropriately.

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:32.510
So inference shows up
all over the place.

00:01:32.510 --> 00:01:38.270
And efficiency, as is
the theme of this class,

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:41.230
really matters more
than ever here.

00:01:41.230 --> 00:01:48.830
And even if you just look at the
actual use app of vantage point,

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:50.550
training is a one-time cost.

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:51.690
It could be very expensive.

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:52.770
It is very expensive.

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:55.150
But once you're done
with it, that's it.

00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:57.900
But inference is
a repeated cost.

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:01.660
You kind of incur
them every single day.

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:05.340
So OpenAI is
estimated to produce

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:09.060
8.6 trillion tokens a day.

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:11.980
And just for
reference, DeepSeek-V4,

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:13.560
which came out
earlier this year,

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:16.380
was trained on 32
trillion tokens.

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:19.060
So in less than four
days, the number of tokens

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:22.380
that OpenAI has to produce,
and therefore the compute

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:26.540
is at least DeepSeek-V4.

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:29.620
And of course, even
the frontier models

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:31.900
might be trained on
more tokens, but still,

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:37.500
inference, the number of tokens
generated is going to be higher.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:40.660
So that's just some perspective.

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:43.860
And moreover, I think the
importance of inference

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:48.580
has grown in the last year
because once upon a time,

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:50.980
we thought of language
models as primarily

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:55.560
as chatbots or assistants,
where you put in a prompt,

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:58.650
you get back a response,
and presumably,

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:01.130
the goal of the human
is to read the response.

00:03:01.130 --> 00:03:04.850
But now, as we move more
into a genetic world,

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:08.050
what it looks like is
that a query goes in,

00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:11.230
and the agent is going
to do a bunch of stuff.

00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:13.470
It's going to think,
it's going to reason,

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:15.970
it's going to call some tools,
it's going to introspect.

00:03:15.970 --> 00:03:18.850
And at the end of the day,
it will produce some output

00:03:18.850 --> 00:03:20.970
for a human to read.

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:23.570
So most of the tokens
that the agent produces

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:27.050
is actually not for reading.

00:03:27.050 --> 00:03:29.210
And so you should really
think about the number

00:03:29.210 --> 00:03:32.490
of tokens generated as
really the compute spend.

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:34.110
And there's no limit.

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:36.290
If you have an ambitious
enough problem,

00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:41.930
you're going to need much
compute and a lot of tokens.

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:43.930
So if you were in
the chatbot world,

00:03:43.930 --> 00:03:47.390
maybe inference after
a certain point,

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:51.450
it's fast enough, because
humans can only read so fast.

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:56.590
But for an agent, there's
no limit to how much a value

00:03:56.590 --> 00:04:00.430
you can get out of squeezing
more out of inference.

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:03.910
So there's a lot of
people doing inference

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:06.310
on the commercial side.

00:04:06.310 --> 00:04:08.830
Of course, all the
closed API providers

00:04:08.830 --> 00:04:11.070
have to serve their
models, so inference

00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:12.870
is a big deal for them.

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:15.550
And there is also a
bunch of providers

00:04:15.550 --> 00:04:20.149
serving open weight models and
providing inference as well.

00:04:20.149 --> 00:04:21.970
And in the open
source community,

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:23.490
there's a bunch of packages.

00:04:23.490 --> 00:04:25.670
So VLM is probably
a popular one.

00:04:25.670 --> 00:04:28.430
Kind of a go-to.

00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:31.590
SG Lang is another one
that's particularly

00:04:31.590 --> 00:04:35.690
good for agentic workloads,
but maybe not as popular yet.

00:04:35.690 --> 00:04:39.770
And then there's TensorRT from
NVIDIA, which is really fast,

00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:42.190
but it's more narrow.

00:04:42.190 --> 00:04:47.710
And then Llama CPP, if you
want to run inference on CPU,

00:04:47.710 --> 00:04:50.630
this is a popular package.

00:04:50.630 --> 00:04:55.540
So hopefully, I've argued
that inference is huge.

00:04:55.540 --> 00:05:00.440
If you can make inference twice
as fast or even 10% faster,

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:02.460
that is a big deal.

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:05.340
So now, the question
is, what does fast mean?

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:08.400
So there's a few metrics that
capture the notion of fast.

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:11.700
And they're going to be
applicable in different settings

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:13.740
and have different trade offs.

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:19.580
So one metric of fast is
time to first token or TTFT.

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:21.900
This is essentially
how long a user waits

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:24.260
before any generation happens.

00:05:24.260 --> 00:05:28.700
So you put in a
query into ChatGPT,

00:05:28.700 --> 00:05:36.740
and some number of milliseconds
passes by, and then

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:40.820
from the point the first token
comes in, that is the time.

00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:43.540
And this is generally useful
for interactive applications

00:05:43.540 --> 00:05:46.900
because that latency where
you're just waiting and doing

00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:48.860
nothing is--

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:51.870
the longer that is, the
worse the user experience,

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:54.210
and as soon as tokens
come in, it doesn't maybe

00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:57.690
have to be that fast, because if
you're going to read it anyway,

00:05:57.690 --> 00:06:00.530
you can't read that fast.

00:06:00.530 --> 00:06:03.650
So the second metric is latency.

00:06:03.650 --> 00:06:06.510
And this is from the standpoint
of an individual user.

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:09.470
How fast are tokens
appearing for one query?

00:06:09.470 --> 00:06:14.330
This is also important for
interactive applications.

00:06:14.330 --> 00:06:17.370
So basically, this is how fast
the tokens are kind of streaming

00:06:17.370 --> 00:06:19.490
through.

00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:21.170
And then the second
related concept

00:06:21.170 --> 00:06:24.570
is throughput, measuring
tokens per second,

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:28.410
this is how fast tokens are
appearing for many queries.

00:06:28.410 --> 00:06:31.930
So latency or one
latency and throughput

00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:34.490
are clearly very related.

00:06:34.490 --> 00:06:38.410
It's very kind of
aligned-- in general,

00:06:38.410 --> 00:06:42.250
many interventions will make
latency and throughput better.

00:06:42.250 --> 00:06:46.930
But as we'll see later, there's
actually a trade off here.

00:06:46.930 --> 00:06:49.360
And throughput is useful
for if you're doing

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:52.080
a bunch of batch processing.

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:54.560
You have a petabyte
of data, and you

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:57.000
want to process it
with a language model.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:59.100
You just want that
job to get done.

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:03.240
It doesn't matter if one query
is coming in faster, sooner,

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:04.960
or later.

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:06.600
OK.

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:09.360
So what determines
the efficiency

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:14.280
by either any of these
metrics of inference.

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:18.240
So the high level
bit is as follows.

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:21.000
And this is-- maybe
one high level bit

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:26.000
to remember from the lecture is
that when you're doing training,

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:28.040
you see all the tokens
at once, because

00:07:28.040 --> 00:07:30.880
in supervised fine-tuning,
you see all the tokens,

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:33.260
and you can parallelize
over the sequence.

00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:35.200
Remember, think about
in the transformer,

00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:38.360
the calculation
for attention, MLP,

00:07:38.360 --> 00:07:40.020
the sequence is
just a dimension.

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:42.920
So it's a big tensor
that gets multiplied.

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:47.150
And so you, essentially,
process all the tokens at once.

00:07:47.150 --> 00:07:50.030
In inference, you can't do that.

00:07:50.030 --> 00:07:52.950
You have to generate because
of the autoregressive nature

00:07:52.950 --> 00:07:54.430
of inference.

00:07:54.430 --> 00:07:55.450
Tokens sequentially.

00:07:55.450 --> 00:07:56.910
One at a time.

00:07:56.910 --> 00:07:59.790
So this is the
fundamental problem

00:07:59.790 --> 00:08:02.870
of why inference is a
very different workload

00:08:02.870 --> 00:08:05.870
than training, because you can't
parallelize across the sequence

00:08:05.870 --> 00:08:07.030
dimension.

00:08:07.030 --> 00:08:08.850
And therefore, as
we'll see later,

00:08:08.850 --> 00:08:12.030
it's going to be harder to
have high arithmetic intensity

00:08:12.030 --> 00:08:15.710
or fully utilize the compute.

00:08:15.710 --> 00:08:18.605
OK.

00:08:18.605 --> 00:08:19.450
All right.

00:08:19.450 --> 00:08:21.370
So let's go on.

00:08:21.370 --> 00:08:24.710
So first, I'm going to-- just
maybe a preview of what's

00:08:24.710 --> 00:08:26.270
in this lecture.

00:08:26.270 --> 00:08:31.670
First, I'm going to do some math
to understand the arithmetic

00:08:31.670 --> 00:08:34.850
intensity and
throughput and latency,

00:08:34.850 --> 00:08:37.549
how to think about
that for a transformer.

00:08:37.549 --> 00:08:40.110
And then I'm going to talk
about various techniques

00:08:40.110 --> 00:08:46.280
to reduce the cost by
making the kv cache smaller,

00:08:46.280 --> 00:08:48.900
quantizing model
pruning, and then

00:08:48.900 --> 00:08:51.260
finally, I'll talk about
speculative decoding, and then

00:08:51.260 --> 00:08:55.460
some kind of practical concerns.

00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:56.000
All right.

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:58.917
So this is going to be a
bit of a review, mostly

00:08:58.917 --> 00:09:00.500
kind of swapping in,
making sure we're

00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:03.060
on the same page with
respect to notation.

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:06.820
Much of this lecture is based
on the scaling book from Google

00:09:06.820 --> 00:09:09.480
on transformers and inference.

00:09:09.480 --> 00:09:11.880
So I do recommend that
people go check it out.

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:13.840
It's a very nicely written.

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:20.060
And some of the
figures are generously

00:09:20.060 --> 00:09:22.660
lifted from that book.

00:09:22.660 --> 00:09:23.620
OK.

00:09:23.620 --> 00:09:27.020
So just to establish
a bit of notation

00:09:27.020 --> 00:09:30.380
to understand this diagram.

00:09:30.380 --> 00:09:33.460
So we're going to use symbols.

00:09:33.460 --> 00:09:37.020
Think about it in ienops
to denote dimensions,

00:09:37.020 --> 00:09:38.640
but also they're kind of length.

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:41.540
So B is going to be the
batch dimension and also

00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:42.893
the number of sequences.

00:09:42.893 --> 00:09:44.810
T is going to be the
sequence dimension, which

00:09:44.810 --> 00:09:46.230
is also the number of tokens.

00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:49.770
D is the model dimension,
H is the dimension.

00:09:49.770 --> 00:09:53.210
So if I'm going to
write this, this

00:09:53.210 --> 00:09:57.930
is taking a product between a
tensor with dimensions B, T,

00:09:57.930 --> 00:10:03.850
and D, and another matrix of
D and H, and I produce BTH.

00:10:03.850 --> 00:10:06.050
So what is going on
here is that there

00:10:06.050 --> 00:10:08.490
are some dimensions
marked red, which

00:10:08.490 --> 00:10:10.290
are going to be contracting.

00:10:10.290 --> 00:10:13.210
These dimensions appear
in both the operands

00:10:13.210 --> 00:10:16.370
and disappear from the results.

00:10:16.370 --> 00:10:19.570
And then there's going to
be other regular dimensions

00:10:19.570 --> 00:10:25.690
in black that appear only in one
operand and stay in the result.

00:10:25.690 --> 00:10:28.050
So this is the general form.

00:10:28.050 --> 00:10:31.730
Another case is if you
have blue dimensions, that

00:10:31.730 --> 00:10:33.890
means they're
attaching dimensions,

00:10:33.890 --> 00:10:36.630
and they appear in both,
but stay in the result.

00:10:36.630 --> 00:10:40.250
They do not get
contracted or reduced.

00:10:40.250 --> 00:10:40.750
OK.

00:10:40.750 --> 00:10:43.220
So the interpretation
here, as we'll see later,

00:10:43.220 --> 00:10:46.560
is that we have this
tensor for every sequence

00:10:46.560 --> 00:10:47.540
and token position.

00:10:47.540 --> 00:10:53.080
You have a vector and you want
to multiply that by a matrix.

00:10:53.080 --> 00:10:55.080
And then here, you're
basically taking

00:10:55.080 --> 00:10:58.900
a bunch of dot products
between two tensors.

00:10:58.900 --> 00:10:59.400
OK.

00:10:59.400 --> 00:11:03.440
So with that in mind, this
in all its full glory,

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:08.380
is the description of
a transformer block.

00:11:08.380 --> 00:11:10.380
So it looks a little bit
like a circuit diagram.

00:11:10.380 --> 00:11:12.640
There's a lot of details
here, but I actually

00:11:12.640 --> 00:11:17.600
find this probably the
most crisp definition

00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:19.480
of what a transformer is.

00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:21.420
You see a lot of
description of transformer,

00:11:21.420 --> 00:11:24.200
and frankly, a lot
of them are-- it's

00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:26.762
hard to understand what
is exactly happening.

00:11:26.762 --> 00:11:28.720
This one basically tells
you exactly the shapes

00:11:28.720 --> 00:11:33.520
of the tensors and allows you
to reason about the dependency.

00:11:33.520 --> 00:11:35.960
So for transformer
block, you take

00:11:35.960 --> 00:11:39.300
the X, which is the
activations of one layer,

00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:41.100
you feed it through
a tension, and then

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:44.020
you feed it through MLP.

00:11:44.020 --> 00:11:50.860
And just as a kind
of a quick refresher,

00:11:50.860 --> 00:11:55.900
you projected using a query
matrix, a key matrix and a value

00:11:55.900 --> 00:12:02.820
matrix, you'll see
here that we have

00:12:02.820 --> 00:12:04.980
N, which is the
number of heads, and H

00:12:04.980 --> 00:12:06.700
is the dimension of each head.

00:12:06.700 --> 00:12:10.740
So the query matrix is batch
times sequence times number

00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:13.060
of heads times the
head dimension,

00:12:13.060 --> 00:12:15.800
and K and V,
instead of having N,

00:12:15.800 --> 00:12:18.620
we have K, which
we'll see later,

00:12:18.620 --> 00:12:23.860
which we have a potentially
smaller number key value heads.

00:12:23.860 --> 00:12:26.820
And then this is the
attention operation,

00:12:26.820 --> 00:12:29.980
where notice that there's
batching dimensions.

00:12:29.980 --> 00:12:32.540
So B appears in both.

00:12:32.540 --> 00:12:36.620
And we're contracting
over the head dimension.

00:12:36.620 --> 00:12:40.890
And we'll come back to
why this B, B in both,

00:12:40.890 --> 00:12:43.410
makes inference hard.

00:12:43.410 --> 00:12:45.690
Why attention is kind
of a bottleneck there.

00:12:45.690 --> 00:12:49.610
So just hold on to that thought.

00:12:49.610 --> 00:12:55.170
And the MLP is very simple
and straightforward.

00:12:55.170 --> 00:12:57.510
You just do some math models.

00:12:57.510 --> 00:13:03.090
This is the gating matrix.

00:13:03.090 --> 00:13:05.230
This is the upper direction
and a down projection.

00:13:05.230 --> 00:13:06.470
So thank you guys.

00:13:06.470 --> 00:13:07.470
I have implemented this.

00:13:07.470 --> 00:13:09.095
So I don't want to
belabor it, but just

00:13:09.095 --> 00:13:11.370
to swap in the notation.

00:13:11.370 --> 00:13:18.050
By convention, we're going
to assume that in the MLP,

00:13:18.050 --> 00:13:22.450
the MLP up projects the D
dimensional, model dimension

00:13:22.450 --> 00:13:23.670
into four times that.

00:13:23.670 --> 00:13:24.930
So F is always--

00:13:24.930 --> 00:13:28.250
think about it as 4D
whenever you see F.

00:13:28.250 --> 00:13:32.150
And then the model dimension
gets split across the N heads.

00:13:32.150 --> 00:13:35.370
So the model dimension is
always equal to number of heads

00:13:35.370 --> 00:13:38.000
times the head dimension.

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:43.040
And then the number
of heads get split,

00:13:43.040 --> 00:13:45.640
in the case of group
query attention,

00:13:45.640 --> 00:13:51.920
into the number of groups and
the number of heads per group.

00:13:51.920 --> 00:13:54.760
And finally, we have
two variables, S and T,

00:13:54.760 --> 00:13:59.160
which both represents
the sequence dimension.

00:13:59.160 --> 00:14:01.080
And the difference
is that S is going

00:14:01.080 --> 00:14:05.520
to represent the
number of input tokens,

00:14:05.520 --> 00:14:08.680
and T is going to represent
the number of output tokens.

00:14:08.680 --> 00:14:10.720
So in training time,
these two are the same,

00:14:10.720 --> 00:14:15.560
because we're predicting all
the same inputs as outputs.

00:14:15.560 --> 00:14:18.000
And inference time,
T is going to be 1,

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:19.660
and S is going to be the input.

00:14:22.960 --> 00:14:23.960
OK.

00:14:23.960 --> 00:14:26.380
So another review for
arithmetic intensity.

00:14:26.380 --> 00:14:28.520
So I talked about this
in the second lecture.

00:14:28.520 --> 00:14:29.520
So just as a warm up.

00:14:29.520 --> 00:14:32.240
Suppose I'm multiplying
two matrices; a B by D

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:34.600
matrix and a D by F matrix.

00:14:34.600 --> 00:14:35.990
So intuition.

00:14:35.990 --> 00:14:38.390
B is the batch dimension,
D is the hidden dimension

00:14:38.390 --> 00:14:41.310
or the model dimension, and
F is the projection dimension

00:14:41.310 --> 00:14:43.470
in the MLP.

00:14:43.470 --> 00:14:47.030
So remember, what are
the arithmetic intensity?

00:14:47.030 --> 00:14:48.990
I have to count
flops, and I have

00:14:48.990 --> 00:14:52.630
to count the amount
of memory moved.

00:14:52.630 --> 00:14:56.150
So we can do this as follows.

00:14:56.150 --> 00:15:01.110
So what do you have to do
to multiply these matrices?

00:15:01.110 --> 00:15:04.550
Now, remember, the
systems, lectures,

00:15:04.550 --> 00:15:08.550
you have to read X from HBM.

00:15:08.550 --> 00:15:10.990
And if you're storing
everything in bf16,

00:15:10.990 --> 00:15:13.870
which is the case
for inference, we're

00:15:13.870 --> 00:15:17.630
going to have 2 times B times D,
you're going to read W. That's

00:15:17.630 --> 00:15:21.070
going to be another
2 times D times F.

00:15:21.070 --> 00:15:22.450
You're going to do the matmul.

00:15:22.450 --> 00:15:29.920
So that's number of flops
is 2 times B times D times F

00:15:29.920 --> 00:15:33.180
And then you're going
to write it back.

00:15:33.180 --> 00:15:33.860
OK?

00:15:33.860 --> 00:15:36.160
So every time you
read and write,

00:15:36.160 --> 00:15:38.880
it's basically the
number of entries.

00:15:38.880 --> 00:15:42.920
It's kind of a quadratic-like
term, and matmuls are qubit.

00:15:42.920 --> 00:15:45.530
And remember, this is
going to be important

00:15:45.530 --> 00:15:47.780
because that's how you're
going to get high arithmetic

00:15:47.780 --> 00:15:48.900
intensity.

00:15:48.900 --> 00:15:53.440
So total number of
flops is the cubic,

00:15:53.440 --> 00:15:57.780
and then the bytes transferred
is the sum of the reads

00:15:57.780 --> 00:15:59.980
and writes.

00:15:59.980 --> 00:16:03.140
So remember, arithmetic
intensity is how much compute

00:16:03.140 --> 00:16:04.920
we do per byte transfer.

00:16:04.920 --> 00:16:06.260
And we want this to be high.

00:16:06.260 --> 00:16:13.020
So the intensity is going
to be equal to flops

00:16:13.020 --> 00:16:14.775
over bytes transfer.

00:16:14.775 --> 00:16:16.900
And I'm representing all
these things symbolically,

00:16:16.900 --> 00:16:20.540
because it will be
easier to see and work

00:16:20.540 --> 00:16:23.780
with rather than numbers.

00:16:23.780 --> 00:16:27.340
One just simplification
we can make

00:16:27.340 --> 00:16:29.820
is that suppose that
the batch dimension here

00:16:29.820 --> 00:16:34.730
is much less than D and F,
then we can simplify this.

00:16:34.730 --> 00:16:38.490
So in particular, what this
is doing is saying D equals C

00:16:38.490 --> 00:16:42.030
times D and F equals C times
B and let C goes to infinity,

00:16:42.030 --> 00:16:45.570
so both D and F are really
large and B is smaller,

00:16:45.570 --> 00:16:51.590
and this just reduces
to the intensity of B.

00:16:51.590 --> 00:16:55.330
So the upshot of
that is that remember

00:16:55.330 --> 00:16:59.490
when we did arithmetic
intensity for matmul,

00:16:59.490 --> 00:17:04.290
it was something like N/3.

00:17:04.290 --> 00:17:07.130
And this is the kind
of analog, where

00:17:07.130 --> 00:17:12.430
instead of just having
square matrices,

00:17:12.430 --> 00:17:14.829
we have non-square matrices.

00:17:14.829 --> 00:17:15.329
OK.

00:17:15.329 --> 00:17:18.930
So just to compare, so
the accelerator intensity

00:17:18.930 --> 00:17:24.410
of a hardware is you look
at the flops per second

00:17:24.410 --> 00:17:28.490
in the spec sheet, you look at
how much memory bandwidth, how

00:17:28.490 --> 00:17:31.920
fast memory gets moved
between HBM, you divide,

00:17:31.920 --> 00:17:35.160
and that is the
accelerated intensity.

00:17:35.160 --> 00:17:40.240
And now, you compare your
computations intensity

00:17:40.240 --> 00:17:42.400
with your accelerator intensity.

00:17:42.400 --> 00:17:47.540
And if it's greater then your
compute bound, which is good,

00:17:47.540 --> 00:17:50.480
if you're less, then you're
memory bound, and that's bad.

00:17:50.480 --> 00:17:54.920
So in this case, for H100,
for this particular matmul

00:17:54.920 --> 00:17:58.760
operation, you're compute
bound if the batch

00:17:58.760 --> 00:18:03.840
size is greater than 295.

00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:06.000
So here's an extreme case.

00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:11.160
So suppose you just
have one example.

00:18:11.160 --> 00:18:13.320
What happens is you
have one example, then

00:18:13.320 --> 00:18:16.320
your arithmetic intensity
is going to be one.

00:18:16.320 --> 00:18:21.040
And this is going
to be memory bound.

00:18:21.040 --> 00:18:23.420
And you can see
what's going on here.

00:18:23.420 --> 00:18:27.140
You're reading this DF
matrix, but B is only one.

00:18:27.140 --> 00:18:31.960
So you're effectively doing
only 2 times D times F flops.

00:18:34.690 --> 00:18:37.020
And this is basically
the workload

00:18:37.020 --> 00:18:38.720
that you'll see in inference.

00:18:38.720 --> 00:18:40.400
You don't get these
full matrices,

00:18:40.400 --> 00:18:45.680
you get these very thin
matrices or tensors.

00:18:45.680 --> 00:18:46.180
OK.

00:18:46.180 --> 00:18:50.100
Let me stop there in case
there's any questions.

00:18:50.100 --> 00:18:51.140
Yeah.

00:18:51.140 --> 00:18:56.512
In the transformer slide, I just
want to confirm [INAUDIBLE].

00:19:03.409 --> 00:19:09.468
You said, across [INAUDIBLE]
can search across [INAUDIBLE]?

00:19:13.340 --> 00:19:17.960
Yeah, should that be across
tables instead of cubes?

00:19:22.180 --> 00:19:22.960
Let's see.

00:19:22.960 --> 00:19:30.170
So the number of
groups here is--

00:19:30.170 --> 00:19:31.790
wait, I'm just making sure.

00:19:31.790 --> 00:19:36.490
So K is the number
key value heads.

00:19:36.490 --> 00:19:45.150
And so you have g per KV head.

00:19:48.010 --> 00:19:54.255
My understanding is [INAUDIBLE]
each key path will have one

00:19:54.255 --> 00:19:59.010
[INAUDIBLE] correspond to it.

00:19:59.010 --> 00:20:06.450
And [INAUDIBLE] then g is
the number of [INAUDIBLE] p.

00:20:06.450 --> 00:20:08.290
That means we have k.

00:20:08.290 --> 00:20:09.130
That's my question.

00:20:09.130 --> 00:20:11.825
Yeah, OK.

00:20:11.825 --> 00:20:12.950
Yeah, I think you're right.

00:20:12.950 --> 00:20:15.810
So k should be the
number of groups,

00:20:15.810 --> 00:20:20.130
and g should be the number
of the heads per one

00:20:20.130 --> 00:20:21.230
of those groups.

00:20:21.230 --> 00:20:21.730
Yes.

00:20:21.730 --> 00:20:22.590
Yeah, OK.

00:20:22.590 --> 00:20:23.310
Thanks for that.

00:20:23.310 --> 00:20:24.270
I'll fix that later.

00:20:27.920 --> 00:20:30.180
OK.

00:20:30.180 --> 00:20:32.560
So let's talk a little bit
more about the arithmetic

00:20:32.560 --> 00:20:34.320
intensity of inference.

00:20:34.320 --> 00:20:41.860
So here's how to think about
what's happening in inference.

00:20:41.860 --> 00:20:44.280
Let's say you just
do it naively.

00:20:44.280 --> 00:20:48.640
So here is a prompt
never going to give you.

00:20:48.640 --> 00:20:50.720
I'm going to the transformer.

00:20:50.720 --> 00:20:55.360
And the transformer does
generates keys and values

00:20:55.360 --> 00:20:56.800
and activations.

00:20:56.800 --> 00:21:00.000
And at the end of the
day, it generates logits

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:02.720
over the output vocabulary.

00:21:02.720 --> 00:21:06.760
And you sample a
token, and that token

00:21:06.760 --> 00:21:09.920
gets concatenated
with the prompt,

00:21:09.920 --> 00:21:11.860
and then you just do this again.

00:21:11.860 --> 00:21:12.960
You generate never.

00:21:12.960 --> 00:21:16.720
You attach that to the prompt
and so on and so forth.

00:21:16.720 --> 00:21:18.620
So this is the most naive thing.

00:21:18.620 --> 00:21:21.920
If you have a black box
that takes a sequence

00:21:21.920 --> 00:21:24.110
and outputs a distribution
over tokens, which

00:21:24.110 --> 00:21:25.650
is exactly what a
transformer does,

00:21:25.650 --> 00:21:28.750
you can just apply
this repeatedly.

00:21:28.750 --> 00:21:36.190
OK, so this works, but this is
really bad because each time you

00:21:36.190 --> 00:21:40.150
generate one token,
you actually take

00:21:40.150 --> 00:21:43.270
order T squared time where
T is the number of tokens

00:21:43.270 --> 00:21:45.130
that you've generated so far.

00:21:45.130 --> 00:21:52.590
So generate T tokens is actually
T cubed because the attention is

00:21:52.590 --> 00:22:00.910
already T squared, and you have
to do that once for every token.

00:22:00.910 --> 00:22:05.830
So this is pretty bad,
but the observation

00:22:05.830 --> 00:22:09.550
is that you don't
actually have to do this.

00:22:09.550 --> 00:22:12.610
A lot of the work can actually
be shared across prefixes.

00:22:12.610 --> 00:22:15.310
So for example, if you're over
generating network versus you're

00:22:15.310 --> 00:22:17.230
generating up, you're
actually computing

00:22:17.230 --> 00:22:24.020
a lot of the same key values
for never going to give.

00:22:24.020 --> 00:22:27.500
So these tokens, they
shouldn't change, OK?

00:22:27.500 --> 00:22:30.040
And this is because it's
a causal transformer.

00:22:30.040 --> 00:22:32.420
If it was bidirectional,
then if you attach a token,

00:22:32.420 --> 00:22:34.060
then everything changes.

00:22:34.060 --> 00:22:36.340
But if it's causal,
then the activations

00:22:36.340 --> 00:22:41.980
here don't change based
on any tokens you append.

00:22:41.980 --> 00:22:45.740
So with that observation,
the first obvious thing

00:22:45.740 --> 00:22:50.500
is to store a KV
cache in HPM, so

00:22:50.500 --> 00:22:52.900
that, between successive
token generations,

00:22:52.900 --> 00:22:55.300
you can just reuse the KV cache.

00:22:55.300 --> 00:23:02.100
So that means when you are
trying to generate gonna, you

00:23:02.100 --> 00:23:05.620
don't actually have to compute
all the keys and activations

00:23:05.620 --> 00:23:10.460
of these previous tokens.

00:23:10.460 --> 00:23:12.680
So this is what it looks
like with a KV cache.

00:23:12.680 --> 00:23:16.640
So there's going to be
two stages pre-fill.

00:23:16.640 --> 00:23:21.010
So you get the prompt, and you
populate the KV cache, which

00:23:21.010 --> 00:23:25.930
is the set key and value pairs
that the transformer computes,

00:23:25.930 --> 00:23:27.750
and then you
generate the logit--

00:23:27.750 --> 00:23:30.330
just the same computation
as we did before.

00:23:30.330 --> 00:23:36.650
And now you have the KV cache
here and then this distribution

00:23:36.650 --> 00:23:38.570
which you sample at a token.

00:23:38.570 --> 00:23:41.490
And now you feed that through
the transformer, which

00:23:41.490 --> 00:23:47.810
uses this cache
and then produces

00:23:47.810 --> 00:23:50.070
both the distribution
of the next tokens,

00:23:50.070 --> 00:23:54.090
but a new KB, which is
basically activations

00:23:54.090 --> 00:23:57.370
corresponding to the token up.

00:23:57.370 --> 00:23:59.410
And then this gets fed in.

00:23:59.410 --> 00:24:02.210
So now you have the
command to KV cache.

00:24:02.210 --> 00:24:05.890
You have this logits you
sample, and then that

00:24:05.890 --> 00:24:07.390
feeds to the transformer.

00:24:07.390 --> 00:24:10.130
You output the distribution
over next tokens

00:24:10.130 --> 00:24:15.250
as well as the new token
which you add to the KB cache

00:24:15.250 --> 00:24:16.310
and so on and so forth.

00:24:19.960 --> 00:24:24.420
So the KV cache formula
is for every sequence--

00:24:24.420 --> 00:24:25.620
so there's B of them.

00:24:25.620 --> 00:24:28.520
For every token,
there's S of them.

00:24:28.520 --> 00:24:32.920
And for every layer
and every head,

00:24:32.920 --> 00:24:34.820
you store H dimensional vector.

00:24:38.120 --> 00:24:41.480
And just to reiterate for
inference, there's prefill.

00:24:41.480 --> 00:24:42.860
You given a prompt.

00:24:42.860 --> 00:24:48.860
You encode the prompt
into these vectors.

00:24:48.860 --> 00:24:51.000
And this is parallelizable
just like in training

00:24:51.000 --> 00:24:52.500
because you see
the entire prompt.

00:24:52.500 --> 00:24:55.240
You can compute the KV cache.

00:24:55.240 --> 00:24:59.560
And then in generation, you
generate the new response tokens

00:24:59.560 --> 00:25:00.600
sequentially.

00:25:00.600 --> 00:25:02.240
But at least you
don't have to pay

00:25:02.240 --> 00:25:05.160
for generating the KV
cache of the tokens

00:25:05.160 --> 00:25:08.200
that you already looked at.

00:25:08.200 --> 00:25:11.300
So is everyone comfortable
with the KV cache?

00:25:11.300 --> 00:25:12.860
Any questions about this?

00:25:22.020 --> 00:25:27.420
All right, so let's try to
figure out the FLOPs and memory

00:25:27.420 --> 00:25:32.100
I/O for both MLP and
attention layers.

00:25:32.100 --> 00:25:33.940
So remember S is
the number of tokens

00:25:33.940 --> 00:25:37.340
we're kind of conditioning on,
and T is the number of tokens

00:25:37.340 --> 00:25:39.260
we're generating.

00:25:39.260 --> 00:25:41.120
So we're going to
do this abstractly.

00:25:41.120 --> 00:25:44.780
And then later, we'll specify
to the prefill where T equals S,

00:25:44.780 --> 00:25:48.740
and generation T equals 1.

00:25:48.740 --> 00:25:51.140
So for the MLP layers--
and through all of this,

00:25:51.140 --> 00:25:52.880
I'm just going to
look at the MAmmoTH

00:25:52.880 --> 00:25:55.140
because the MAmmoTH
are the thing that

00:25:55.140 --> 00:25:57.920
actually require a lot of work.

00:25:57.920 --> 00:26:02.180
Everything else can
is not that many FLOPs

00:26:02.180 --> 00:26:06.760
and can be potentially
fused into the MAmmoTH too.

00:26:06.760 --> 00:26:07.260
OK.

00:26:07.260 --> 00:26:09.440
So let's do this calculation.

00:26:09.440 --> 00:26:12.000
So it's the same as the
matrix calculations.

00:26:12.000 --> 00:26:15.890
I'm not going to maybe belabored
step through every single detail

00:26:15.890 --> 00:26:19.610
here, but just the
form is you read X

00:26:19.610 --> 00:26:24.130
from high bandwidth memory.

00:26:24.130 --> 00:26:27.570
You read all the parameters.

00:26:27.570 --> 00:26:33.130
You compute the
upper projection,

00:26:33.130 --> 00:26:35.610
and then you write it to HBM.

00:26:35.610 --> 00:26:42.850
You compute the gate,
and you write HBM,

00:26:42.850 --> 00:26:51.050
and then you compute basically
the down projection of that,

00:26:51.050 --> 00:26:53.970
and you write it to HBM.

00:26:53.970 --> 00:26:59.690
So the number of flops
depends on B and T

00:26:59.690 --> 00:27:02.970
and D and F. So batch
size, sequence length,

00:27:02.970 --> 00:27:07.170
model dimension, and the
feedforward dimension,

00:27:07.170 --> 00:27:08.450
MLP dimension.

00:27:08.450 --> 00:27:15.040
And the bytes transferred
is this expression.

00:27:15.040 --> 00:27:17.640
So now you can compute
the arithmetic intensity

00:27:17.640 --> 00:27:22.200
which, again, is FLOPs
divided by bytes transferred.

00:27:22.200 --> 00:27:24.400
So this is some expression.

00:27:24.400 --> 00:27:27.440
We're going to assume, that
just like before, B times T

00:27:27.440 --> 00:27:33.720
is much smaller than D and
F. And with that, then we see

00:27:33.720 --> 00:27:38.840
that intensity is B times T.

00:27:38.840 --> 00:27:41.900
So this is analogous to
just the MAmmoTH case.

00:27:41.900 --> 00:27:44.380
There's more matrices and
there's more dimensions,

00:27:44.380 --> 00:27:46.225
but fundamentally
it's the same thing.

00:27:46.225 --> 00:27:47.100
And that makes sense.

00:27:47.100 --> 00:27:50.620
An MLP is basically
a big MAmmoTH.

00:27:50.620 --> 00:27:55.760
And it also makes sense
because the batch dimension

00:27:55.760 --> 00:27:59.660
and the sequence
length dimension are--

00:27:59.660 --> 00:28:01.840
everything is independent.

00:28:01.840 --> 00:28:04.860
And for the MLP,
they don't interact.

00:28:04.860 --> 00:28:06.600
Attention is different story.

00:28:06.600 --> 00:28:12.350
And so B times T. So
as long as you have--

00:28:12.350 --> 00:28:15.270
in the pre-fill, if
you make B times T

00:28:15.270 --> 00:28:19.030
large enough-- large batches,
long sequences-- you'll be fine.

00:28:19.030 --> 00:28:20.690
Now, let's look at generation.

00:28:20.690 --> 00:28:24.430
So remember, generation,
there's two problems here.

00:28:24.430 --> 00:28:28.930
One is that
generation T equals 1.

00:28:28.930 --> 00:28:33.030
You're only generating
one token at a time.

00:28:33.030 --> 00:28:35.710
And so that means your
arithmetic intensity

00:28:35.710 --> 00:28:40.230
is going to be B,
but B in generation

00:28:40.230 --> 00:28:43.270
is the number of
concurrent requests.

00:28:43.270 --> 00:28:46.730
And so if you're in a batch
setting, you can control that.

00:28:46.730 --> 00:28:48.967
But if you're, let's
say, serving a chatbot,

00:28:48.967 --> 00:28:50.550
it's the number of
concurrent requests

00:28:50.550 --> 00:28:55.150
is essentially how many users,
concurrent users there are?

00:28:55.150 --> 00:28:56.170
Which can be high.

00:28:56.170 --> 00:28:56.850
It be low.

00:28:56.850 --> 00:28:58.330
So it's a bit unpredictable.

00:28:58.330 --> 00:28:59.547
It can be changing over time.

00:28:59.547 --> 00:29:01.630
So that's something we're
going to address when we

00:29:01.630 --> 00:29:06.630
talk about continuous batching.

00:29:06.630 --> 00:29:10.980
But overall, this is
not bad because as long

00:29:10.980 --> 00:29:14.388
as you have large batches,
the sequence length

00:29:14.388 --> 00:29:15.680
isn't going to really help you.

00:29:15.680 --> 00:29:18.860
But if you have large
batches, you should be good.

00:29:18.860 --> 00:29:22.460
So now let's look at
the attention layer.

00:29:22.460 --> 00:29:25.760
So again, S is in the previous
tokens already generated.

00:29:25.760 --> 00:29:29.900
T is the number of tokens you
want to generate logits for.

00:29:29.900 --> 00:29:40.500
So in attention, you read
the QKV matrices from HPM.

00:29:40.500 --> 00:29:43.580
You compute the attention.

00:29:43.580 --> 00:29:47.380
You compute the softmax
which doesn't really matter,

00:29:47.380 --> 00:29:51.820
and compute the value
matrix, and then you

00:29:51.820 --> 00:29:54.460
write the result to HBM.

00:29:54.460 --> 00:29:59.020
So the FLOPs is B times
S times T times D,

00:29:59.020 --> 00:30:04.420
and the bytes transferred
is this expression.

00:30:04.420 --> 00:30:07.830
Everything's is
a MAmmoTH, right?

00:30:07.830 --> 00:30:13.650
So this should be always one
degree higher of a polynomial

00:30:13.650 --> 00:30:17.450
than the bytes transferred
because it's a MAmmoTH.

00:30:17.450 --> 00:30:21.850
The only question is, what
is that factor look like?

00:30:21.850 --> 00:30:29.570
So that factor for attention
is S times T over S plus T.

00:30:29.570 --> 00:30:33.170
So let's look at the prefill.

00:30:33.170 --> 00:30:37.210
So prefill, what
this is saying is

00:30:37.210 --> 00:30:41.970
that the prefill intensity
is S over 2 when T equals

00:30:41.970 --> 00:30:44.270
S. So this is good, right?

00:30:44.270 --> 00:30:49.210
Because as long as you have long
sequence lengths for attention,

00:30:49.210 --> 00:30:54.890
you're going to maintain
high arithmetic intensity.

00:30:54.890 --> 00:30:58.190
Notice that the batching
dimension doesn't happen here.

00:30:58.190 --> 00:31:02.330
I'll explain a bit why later.

00:31:02.330 --> 00:31:07.360
But for generation,
this is bad news.

00:31:07.360 --> 00:31:12.200
So the generated nation
intensity is S over S plus 1,

00:31:12.200 --> 00:31:16.960
and that's less than 1, or
even let's just call it 1.

00:31:16.960 --> 00:31:19.340
And aromatic intensity,
remember 1 is bad.

00:31:19.340 --> 00:31:22.120
We want it to be something
like 295 for H100

00:31:22.120 --> 00:31:24.520
to be to saturate the compute.

00:31:24.520 --> 00:31:29.860
And so this is really
the bottleneck.

00:31:29.860 --> 00:31:35.680
So we did all this
analysis for MLP attention,

00:31:35.680 --> 00:31:39.640
prefilled generation, and we've
found this to be a bottleneck.

00:31:39.640 --> 00:31:46.400
So let's try to contemplate
why this is a bottleneck.

00:31:46.400 --> 00:31:50.800
So unlike MLPs-- so MLP is
for generation is actually OK

00:31:50.800 --> 00:31:52.760
as long as your sequence is--

00:31:52.760 --> 00:31:59.080
sorry, your batch
is large enough.

00:31:59.080 --> 00:32:07.790
So the problem with this
ML attention is that--

00:32:07.790 --> 00:32:09.430
let's look at MLPs.

00:32:09.430 --> 00:32:14.670
Every sequence hits
the same MLP weights.

00:32:14.670 --> 00:32:19.830
So these don't depend on B.
Whereas, in attention layer,

00:32:19.830 --> 00:32:23.390
each sequence has
its own KV cache.

00:32:23.390 --> 00:32:27.830
So these all depend on MLP.

00:32:27.830 --> 00:32:36.230
And so you can think about
this as what's going on here

00:32:36.230 --> 00:32:43.070
is that in the MLP case,
having B being big actually

00:32:43.070 --> 00:32:48.510
is helpful because you get to
load these MLP weights once.

00:32:48.510 --> 00:32:52.230
And you can use it for
all your sequences.

00:32:52.230 --> 00:32:54.830
That's how you get high
arithmetic intensity

00:32:54.830 --> 00:32:56.630
because you use it--

00:32:56.630 --> 00:32:58.510
simplifying a bit,
you load it once.

00:32:58.510 --> 00:32:59.990
You do all your
batch processing,

00:32:59.990 --> 00:33:02.790
and then that is good.

00:33:02.790 --> 00:33:09.010
Whereas, in attention, all these
depend on B. So increasing B

00:33:09.010 --> 00:33:09.950
doesn't help.

00:33:09.950 --> 00:33:12.810
It's like for every
sequence, you're

00:33:12.810 --> 00:33:15.490
basically doing a MAmmoTH.

00:33:15.490 --> 00:33:17.430
So they're all independent.

00:33:17.430 --> 00:33:21.890
So doing more MAmmoTHs
isn't helpful.

00:33:21.890 --> 00:33:24.750
Just if you remember
in the very beginning,

00:33:24.750 --> 00:33:27.250
I showed you this example.

00:33:27.250 --> 00:33:31.270
This also has pretty bad
arithmetic intensity.

00:33:31.270 --> 00:33:33.490
It's not a MAmmoTH
because we're essentially

00:33:33.490 --> 00:33:37.130
batching by a coordinate.

00:33:37.130 --> 00:33:39.210
And this is essentially
basically the same

00:33:39.210 --> 00:33:43.370
as doing a dot product,
which has horrible arithmetic

00:33:43.370 --> 00:33:44.210
intensity.

00:33:44.210 --> 00:33:46.430
And remember, this
is in the attention.

00:33:46.430 --> 00:33:52.210
This blue B is the cause
of why the attention is

00:33:52.210 --> 00:33:54.410
arithmetic intensity
doesn't scale with B,

00:33:54.410 --> 00:33:58.290
and why that is a bottleneck.

00:33:58.290 --> 00:34:00.170
So just to summarize here.

00:34:00.170 --> 00:34:02.260
So prefill is compute-bound.

00:34:02.260 --> 00:34:04.640
Generation is memory-bound.

00:34:04.640 --> 00:34:10.560
So if you look at the MLP
intensity for prefill,

00:34:10.560 --> 00:34:12.480
it's B times S, great.

00:34:12.480 --> 00:34:18.040
Prefill for attention,
intensity S over 2--

00:34:18.040 --> 00:34:20.400
not as good, but workable.

00:34:20.400 --> 00:34:24.040
Generation MLP
intensity, also workable,

00:34:24.040 --> 00:34:26.679
requires long
concurrent requests.

00:34:26.679 --> 00:34:29.920
But it is really the
generation attention

00:34:29.920 --> 00:34:36.880
intensity which is a fundamental
bottleneck, and that's it.

00:34:36.880 --> 00:34:38.659
If you're sticking
with a transformer,

00:34:38.659 --> 00:34:43.639
you can't really improve this.

00:34:43.639 --> 00:34:44.880
OK.

00:34:44.880 --> 00:34:46.860
Pause there for questions.

00:34:57.560 --> 00:35:00.550
So now whenever you hear
people say, oh, inference

00:35:00.550 --> 00:35:03.510
is memory bound, you know why.

00:35:07.070 --> 00:35:07.750
OK.

00:35:07.750 --> 00:35:13.270
So let's now use these
intuitions and calculations

00:35:13.270 --> 00:35:17.550
to think about our
inference metrics

00:35:17.550 --> 00:35:21.230
throughput and
latency and also TFRT.

00:35:21.230 --> 00:35:21.730
All right.

00:35:21.730 --> 00:35:24.410
So inference is memory bound.

00:35:27.310 --> 00:35:30.110
Now the main-- I
mean, in some ways

00:35:30.110 --> 00:35:32.310
this simplifies a lot
of things because when

00:35:32.310 --> 00:35:34.150
we think about how
long things take,

00:35:34.150 --> 00:35:38.590
you just look at how much memory
needs to be transferred, OK?

00:35:38.590 --> 00:35:44.230
Because assuming you overlap
communication and computation,

00:35:44.230 --> 00:35:47.630
the bottleneck is going to
be just the amount of memory

00:35:47.630 --> 00:35:49.470
that you have to deal with.

00:35:49.470 --> 00:35:52.170
So in some ways, it's
nice because it's simpler.

00:35:52.170 --> 00:35:54.710
But in other ways,
it's frustrating

00:35:54.710 --> 00:35:57.260
that your accelerators
are sitting there not

00:35:57.260 --> 00:35:58.080
doing anything.

00:36:00.660 --> 00:36:04.240
So let's walk
through an example.

00:36:04.240 --> 00:36:09.460
So for Llama 2.13B on an H100.

00:36:09.460 --> 00:36:12.500
So what is the latency
and the throughput?

00:36:12.500 --> 00:36:16.960
So remember Llama 2.13B
has a particular shape.

00:36:16.960 --> 00:36:21.060
So this is a sequence
length, the model dimension,

00:36:21.060 --> 00:36:25.560
feedforward dimension, number of
query, number key value heads.

00:36:25.560 --> 00:36:26.760
There's no GQA here.

00:36:26.760 --> 00:36:29.700
So the N equals
k, head dimension,

00:36:29.700 --> 00:36:34.500
number of layers of vocab
size, and the memory bandwidth

00:36:34.500 --> 00:36:41.820
for H100.

00:36:41.820 --> 00:36:46.740
So now, using this
config, let's compute

00:36:46.740 --> 00:36:50.960
the transformer
performance statistics, OK?

00:36:50.960 --> 00:36:55.450
So these are our inputs here.

00:36:55.450 --> 00:36:58.930
And just the statistics
I'm going to compute

00:36:58.930 --> 00:37:05.570
are going to be number of
parameters, the memory, usage

00:37:05.570 --> 00:37:07.810
latency, and throughput.

00:37:07.810 --> 00:37:16.290
So first of all,
what takes memory?

00:37:16.290 --> 00:37:18.610
So the parameters take memory.

00:37:18.610 --> 00:37:23.170
So we compute the
number of parameters.

00:37:23.170 --> 00:37:29.930
And so you look at the
embeddings, the MLP layers,

00:37:29.930 --> 00:37:34.010
the projections of the KQV.

00:37:34.010 --> 00:37:39.450
At the end of the day, you
get some number of parameters.

00:37:39.450 --> 00:37:44.370
Assuming that we're doing
a bf16, which is always

00:37:44.370 --> 00:37:46.490
the case for inference,
then parameters

00:37:46.490 --> 00:37:49.330
take up this many bytes.

00:37:49.330 --> 00:37:53.280
Also in memory is the KV cache.

00:37:53.280 --> 00:37:58.800
And the KV cache is the number
of tokens in your sequence

00:37:58.800 --> 00:38:07.200
times the number of heads and
the KV heads times the head

00:38:07.200 --> 00:38:09.200
dimension times the
number of layers.

00:38:09.200 --> 00:38:12.780
You have one for the key
and one for the value,

00:38:12.780 --> 00:38:16.580
and then you have
multiple two for bf16.

00:38:16.580 --> 00:38:18.960
So that's the size
of the KV cache.

00:38:18.960 --> 00:38:21.720
And the total memory usage is--

00:38:21.720 --> 00:38:24.740
this is for each sequence
you have B sequences.

00:38:24.740 --> 00:38:28.120
So that's B times that
plus the parameter size.

00:38:28.120 --> 00:38:32.000
And that's the amount
of memory you need.

00:38:32.000 --> 00:38:34.300
And now, what's the latency?

00:38:34.300 --> 00:38:41.520
The latency is determined
by the memory I/O, OK?

00:38:41.520 --> 00:38:46.160
Because inference is
memory-bound, most assuming you

00:38:46.160 --> 00:38:49.720
overlap communication
and compute.

00:38:49.720 --> 00:38:56.510
All the memory, it's going to
be on shuffling parameters back

00:38:56.510 --> 00:39:01.670
and forth between HPM and SRAM.

00:39:01.670 --> 00:39:06.870
So that's how long it takes
to move memory around.

00:39:06.870 --> 00:39:09.690
Throughput is the
inverse of latency,

00:39:09.690 --> 00:39:12.210
but we're generating
B tokens in parallel.

00:39:12.210 --> 00:39:16.170
So this is tokens per second.

00:39:16.170 --> 00:39:17.290
This is seconds per token.

00:39:17.290 --> 00:39:19.830
This is tokens per
second, as well as

00:39:19.830 --> 00:39:27.290
having an additional B because
you're processing a batch of B.

00:39:27.290 --> 00:39:31.870
So now, let's compute
for this config what

00:39:31.870 --> 00:39:33.590
are these actual values?

00:39:33.590 --> 00:39:39.390
So num parameters is 13 billion.

00:39:39.390 --> 00:39:42.510
So a good sanity check-- that
is advertised as a 13 billion

00:39:42.510 --> 00:39:44.390
parameter model.

00:39:44.390 --> 00:39:53.090
Memory is this term, which
is about 838 million times B.

00:39:53.090 --> 00:39:55.650
So it's basically
a linear function

00:39:55.650 --> 00:39:58.510
of B plus some other term.

00:39:58.510 --> 00:40:00.570
So this is a KV
cache which grows

00:40:00.570 --> 00:40:02.930
as B. This is the
parameters, which

00:40:02.930 --> 00:40:06.370
is double the number of params.

00:40:06.370 --> 00:40:10.630
Now, latency is just this
scale by the memory bandwidth,

00:40:10.630 --> 00:40:15.050
so it has the same
form as the memory.

00:40:15.050 --> 00:40:17.430
And throughput is B over that.

00:40:20.850 --> 00:40:24.610
So notice that latency,
as you increase B,

00:40:24.610 --> 00:40:28.630
grows because the
KV cache grows.

00:40:28.630 --> 00:40:30.090
And in order to
process stuff, you

00:40:30.090 --> 00:40:33.450
have to copy the KV
cache back and forth.

00:40:33.450 --> 00:40:35.930
And now, throughput
is more interesting

00:40:35.930 --> 00:40:39.250
because, as you
increase B, throughput

00:40:39.250 --> 00:40:45.250
does improve but up to a limit.

00:40:45.250 --> 00:40:47.530
Throughput improves
because now you're

00:40:47.530 --> 00:40:50.760
amortizing the cost
over a larger batch.

00:40:50.760 --> 00:40:54.280
But also, the speed at which
you're processing this obviously

00:40:54.280 --> 00:40:58.480
is also increasing over time.

00:40:58.480 --> 00:41:00.720
So this asymptotes.

00:41:00.720 --> 00:41:01.600
It's not going to--

00:41:01.600 --> 00:41:03.980
throughput can't
possibly go to infinity.

00:41:06.600 --> 00:41:10.820
Any questions about this so far?

00:41:14.360 --> 00:41:16.720
So basically, you
just have to remember

00:41:16.720 --> 00:41:21.320
latency is a linear function in
B. This is where the constant is

00:41:21.320 --> 00:41:22.880
the size of KV
cache, and then this

00:41:22.880 --> 00:41:24.160
is the number of parameters.

00:41:24.160 --> 00:41:30.720
And then throughput is some
proportional to B over B

00:41:30.720 --> 00:41:31.820
plus something.

00:41:35.040 --> 00:41:40.140
So now, let's instantiate this
for a bunch of situations, OK?

00:41:40.140 --> 00:41:46.280
So if you have batch size 1,
so this is what you would get.

00:41:46.280 --> 00:41:55.470
You get a latency of
0.008 seconds per token.

00:41:55.470 --> 00:41:59.110
And the throughput is
124 tokens per second.

00:41:59.110 --> 00:42:02.070
So what happens if you
increase the batch size,

00:42:02.070 --> 00:42:07.470
you'll see that the
latency goes up,

00:42:07.470 --> 00:42:09.330
but the throughput also goes up.

00:42:09.330 --> 00:42:13.070
So latency gets worse, but
the throughput improves.

00:42:13.070 --> 00:42:16.710
So this is an interesting thing
because we think about, oh, we

00:42:16.710 --> 00:42:18.370
just want to make it go fast.

00:42:18.370 --> 00:42:19.910
But fast actually
has two meanings

00:42:19.910 --> 00:42:22.870
here, which actually, depending
on which one you care about,

00:42:22.870 --> 00:42:24.610
is completely opposite.

00:42:24.610 --> 00:42:27.110
If you want to tune
your batch size,

00:42:27.110 --> 00:42:30.670
that's going to really
determine if you

00:42:30.670 --> 00:42:33.630
want latency or throughput.

00:42:33.630 --> 00:42:36.710
And what happens if you
increase-- let's say, increase--

00:42:36.710 --> 00:42:38.790
we really want high
throughput because we're

00:42:38.790 --> 00:42:40.410
processing lots of documents.

00:42:40.410 --> 00:42:42.970
Let's increase the batch
size even more, OK?

00:42:42.970 --> 00:42:45.620
So the latency gets even worse.

00:42:45.620 --> 00:42:47.380
I mean, yeah, it gets worse.

00:42:47.380 --> 00:42:49.580
Throughput gets even better.

00:42:49.580 --> 00:42:53.900
But the main problem here is
that your memory, you run out

00:42:53.900 --> 00:42:59.660
of memory because the memory,
for storing this KV cache,

00:42:59.660 --> 00:43:03.820
is going to exceed
your H100 memory.

00:43:03.820 --> 00:43:06.440
And if you have B200s, you can
increase the batch size more,

00:43:06.440 --> 00:43:09.180
but eventually you
hit some of limit.

00:43:09.180 --> 00:43:11.780
So there's limitations
to how much

00:43:11.780 --> 00:43:13.320
you can increase
your throughput.

00:43:13.320 --> 00:43:15.980
You'll never get
to the asymptote

00:43:15.980 --> 00:43:20.700
because you'll hit the memory.

00:43:20.700 --> 00:43:24.940
And also, your
throughput is getting--

00:43:24.940 --> 00:43:27.400
your gains are kind of
diminishing as well.

00:43:30.780 --> 00:43:34.780
So increasing batch
size worsens the latency

00:43:34.780 --> 00:43:40.260
because now you have a larger
KV cache to read and write.

00:43:40.260 --> 00:43:41.980
And remember, it's batched.

00:43:41.980 --> 00:43:43.750
So if you're an
individual query,

00:43:43.750 --> 00:43:45.490
you have to wait for
everyone to finish.

00:43:45.490 --> 00:43:48.650
So you have to basically--
you're waiting for a bus,

00:43:48.650 --> 00:43:51.050
and the latency is pretty high.

00:43:51.050 --> 00:43:54.170
You wait and then you go.

00:43:54.170 --> 00:43:56.210
Whereas, the throughput
of a bus is pretty good

00:43:56.210 --> 00:43:59.930
because you can move
everyone at once.

00:43:59.930 --> 00:44:04.190
So the throughput improves
as batch size increases

00:44:04.190 --> 00:44:08.190
because, remember, the
parameters are shared,

00:44:08.190 --> 00:44:12.070
and you load that
once into memory.

00:44:12.070 --> 00:44:14.630
And you can process
a lot of sequences.

00:44:17.330 --> 00:44:20.290
So trade-off between
latency and throughput,

00:44:20.290 --> 00:44:23.210
just to make sure everyone's
aligned-- smaller batch sizes

00:44:23.210 --> 00:44:25.430
use better latency
but worse throughput.

00:44:25.430 --> 00:44:29.770
Large batch sizes yield better
throughput but worse latency.

00:44:29.770 --> 00:44:30.970
OK.

00:44:30.970 --> 00:44:33.630
I'm not going to really talk
about parallelism too much.

00:44:33.630 --> 00:44:35.510
There's also another
dimension of inference,

00:44:35.510 --> 00:44:39.530
which is you can
shard your model

00:44:39.530 --> 00:44:43.000
across multiple all devices.

00:44:43.000 --> 00:44:45.440
You can look at the scaling
book chapter on inference

00:44:45.440 --> 00:44:47.600
if you want to know more.

00:44:47.600 --> 00:44:50.680
Just as a very trivial
example, if you

00:44:50.680 --> 00:44:53.760
launch M copies of the model,
the latency is the same,

00:44:53.760 --> 00:44:58.760
and the throughput
increases by M.

00:44:58.760 --> 00:45:01.000
And then the other metric
we didn't talk about

00:45:01.000 --> 00:45:02.880
is time to first token.

00:45:02.880 --> 00:45:04.760
And this is
essentially the time it

00:45:04.760 --> 00:45:07.840
takes to do prefill because
after you finish prefill,

00:45:07.840 --> 00:45:11.840
then you can basically
start generating.

00:45:11.840 --> 00:45:20.680
So if you want to fast--

00:45:20.680 --> 00:45:26.360
if you want faster TTFT, you
should use smaller batch sizes,

00:45:26.360 --> 00:45:30.560
and you want larger batch
sizes to improve throughput.

00:45:30.560 --> 00:45:34.820
So hopefully, that is clear.

00:45:34.820 --> 00:45:37.320
Any questions about
throughput and latency

00:45:37.320 --> 00:45:39.420
and how their intention
with each other?

00:45:47.830 --> 00:45:48.630
OK.

00:45:48.630 --> 00:45:51.510
So now that we have a conceptual
framework for thinking

00:45:51.510 --> 00:45:57.830
about how efficient inference is
in terms of arithmetic intensity

00:45:57.830 --> 00:46:03.810
and throughput and latency, now
let's try to make it faster.

00:46:03.810 --> 00:46:05.210
How do we make inference faster?

00:46:05.210 --> 00:46:07.830
There's a bunch of
different techniques which

00:46:07.830 --> 00:46:11.150
are quite varied, ranging from
changing the model architecture

00:46:11.150 --> 00:46:13.690
to doing systems optimization
and everything in between.

00:46:13.690 --> 00:46:20.350
So inference in some sense is a
fairly rich cross-cutting topic.

00:46:20.350 --> 00:46:27.550
So the most maybe now in
hindsight obvious thing

00:46:27.550 --> 00:46:31.430
you can think about is
hopefully beat it into your head

00:46:31.430 --> 00:46:35.630
that memory is the
bottleneck for inference,

00:46:35.630 --> 00:46:39.050
and the KV cache takes
up a lot of memory.

00:46:39.050 --> 00:46:42.410
And it could even be larger
than the number of parameters

00:46:42.410 --> 00:46:44.330
if for a large
enough batch size.

00:46:44.330 --> 00:46:48.450
So let's just try to reduce
the size of the KV cache.

00:46:48.450 --> 00:46:50.810
Now, you have to be careful
about how you do this

00:46:50.810 --> 00:46:52.810
because you want to make
sure you don't lose too

00:46:52.810 --> 00:46:56.050
much accuracy in the process.

00:46:56.050 --> 00:47:00.750
So here is one thing you can do,
which we already talked about,

00:47:00.750 --> 00:47:03.450
which is grouped
query attention.

00:47:03.450 --> 00:47:09.490
And just as a reminder here,
so multi-headed attention--

00:47:09.490 --> 00:47:11.250
basically for every
token, you have

00:47:11.250 --> 00:47:13.850
a key and a value and a query.

00:47:13.850 --> 00:47:16.930
And if you do grouped
query attention,

00:47:16.930 --> 00:47:20.610
you basically compute the
same number of queries,

00:47:20.610 --> 00:47:24.570
but you have only a
smaller number of groups,

00:47:24.570 --> 00:47:28.930
and you compute key and
value for each group.

00:47:28.930 --> 00:47:36.680
So K is the number
of groups here.

00:47:36.680 --> 00:47:41.560
And so in the multi-headed
attention, K is N, no reduction.

00:47:41.560 --> 00:47:44.200
There's something called multi
query attention, which no one

00:47:44.200 --> 00:47:46.120
uses because it's really bad.

00:47:46.120 --> 00:47:47.440
K equals 1.

00:47:47.440 --> 00:47:50.280
And somewhere in
between is hopefully

00:47:50.280 --> 00:47:54.920
where we'll find a balance
between accuracy and speed.

00:47:54.920 --> 00:47:59.720
So this is the paper that
introduces GQA from 2023.

00:47:59.720 --> 00:48:05.600
And they show that if you
look at time per sample, which

00:48:05.600 --> 00:48:09.920
is related to both
latency and throughput,

00:48:09.920 --> 00:48:15.480
you see that the MHA--
so Multi-Head Attention.

00:48:15.480 --> 00:48:20.080
Full attention has
this high time.

00:48:20.080 --> 00:48:24.880
Whereas, if you start K
equals 1, it's much faster.

00:48:24.880 --> 00:48:29.118
And then you can actually
keep on increasing the K to K

00:48:29.118 --> 00:48:30.660
equals 8, and it's
still pretty good.

00:48:30.660 --> 00:48:36.910
And eventually, your
time goes up quite a bit.

00:48:36.910 --> 00:48:39.830
OK.

00:48:39.830 --> 00:48:43.950
So why does GQA improve
latency and throughput?

00:48:43.950 --> 00:48:53.190
Well, it reduces the KV cache by
a factor of N over K. And just

00:48:53.190 --> 00:48:58.190
as a friendly reminder, reducing
memory usage leads to speed up

00:48:58.190 --> 00:49:00.270
because we're memory-bound.

00:49:00.270 --> 00:49:06.767
So let's revisit our
friendly Llama model here.

00:49:06.767 --> 00:49:08.350
Remember in the
initial configuration,

00:49:08.350 --> 00:49:11.230
we're just using K
equals N. So there's

00:49:11.230 --> 00:49:15.110
no multi-headed-- there's
multi-head attention,

00:49:15.110 --> 00:49:19.110
no reduction in the
number key and values.

00:49:19.110 --> 00:49:25.030
And so for this one, remember
using a batch size of 64,

00:49:25.030 --> 00:49:27.510
we get this throughput
and latency.

00:49:27.510 --> 00:49:32.370
Now if you do GQA, we're
going to put in, let's say,

00:49:32.370 --> 00:49:36.260
a kind of a sparsity
of 1 one five.

00:49:36.260 --> 00:49:44.580
That reduces the
memory quite a bit,

00:49:44.580 --> 00:49:47.060
which, in turn,
improves the latency

00:49:47.060 --> 00:49:49.740
and also improves
the throughput.

00:49:49.740 --> 00:49:52.780
So it's not that latency and
throughput are always at odds.

00:49:52.780 --> 00:49:56.540
If you reduce the amount of
memory, then it improves both.

00:49:56.540 --> 00:49:59.420
It's mainly the batch
dimension that allows.

00:49:59.420 --> 00:50:01.800
That is the point of tension.

00:50:01.800 --> 00:50:02.300
OK.

00:50:02.300 --> 00:50:06.220
So this is great.

00:50:06.220 --> 00:50:13.540
And let's actually just increase
the batch size even more

00:50:13.540 --> 00:50:15.860
and see what happens.

00:50:15.860 --> 00:50:20.780
So before, if we had
a batch size of 256,

00:50:20.780 --> 00:50:22.240
we ran out of memory.

00:50:22.240 --> 00:50:24.340
But now, it fits in memory.

00:50:24.340 --> 00:50:32.690
And we see that, the latency
suffers a bit because we're

00:50:32.690 --> 00:50:36.570
increasing the batch
size, but the throughput

00:50:36.570 --> 00:50:40.770
goes up proportionally.

00:50:40.770 --> 00:50:45.310
So sometimes you play with
these parameters jointly.

00:50:45.310 --> 00:50:47.090
You can reduce the
KV cache, but that

00:50:47.090 --> 00:50:48.850
allows you to increase
the batch size

00:50:48.850 --> 00:50:52.810
and allow making
other trade-offs.

00:50:52.810 --> 00:50:55.010
So the final thing you
have to do whenever

00:50:55.010 --> 00:50:58.130
you do some lossy
change is that you make

00:50:58.130 --> 00:50:59.970
sure the accuracy doesn't drop.

00:50:59.970 --> 00:51:08.470
And this paper shows that for
a GQA, the time is better.

00:51:08.470 --> 00:51:14.850
But across a bunch of evals,
basically it works well.

00:51:14.850 --> 00:51:16.538
So now with these
accuracy values,

00:51:16.538 --> 00:51:18.830
I think you always have to
take it with a grain of salt

00:51:18.830 --> 00:51:20.790
because this is for
a particular model.

00:51:20.790 --> 00:51:24.390
Later, the deep sea paper should
show that it actually does hurt.

00:51:24.390 --> 00:51:28.530
So I guess, take
everything that's

00:51:28.530 --> 00:51:32.760
not just math with a
grain of salt here.

00:51:32.760 --> 00:51:33.260
OK.

00:51:33.260 --> 00:51:36.320
So speaking of DeepSeek,
here's another idea

00:51:36.320 --> 00:51:39.880
to reduce the KV cache.

00:51:39.880 --> 00:51:43.720
So the theme is reduce the KV
cache and latency and throughput

00:51:43.720 --> 00:51:45.760
improve.

00:51:45.760 --> 00:51:49.280
So this is
multi-headed attention,

00:51:49.280 --> 00:51:54.320
same number of queries and keys
and values for every token.

00:51:54.320 --> 00:51:58.600
And GQA, remember, we have
reduced the number of keys

00:51:58.600 --> 00:52:01.480
and values for every token.

00:52:01.480 --> 00:52:02.620
Sorry, not for every token.

00:52:02.620 --> 00:52:07.280
We basically have reduced the
number of values and keys.

00:52:07.280 --> 00:52:11.440
And now, the multi-latent
attention for DeepSeek

00:52:11.440 --> 00:52:15.960
says we're actually going
to leave the number of keys

00:52:15.960 --> 00:52:21.148
and values the same one for
every token essentially.

00:52:21.148 --> 00:52:22.440
But I'm going to parameterize--

00:52:22.440 --> 00:52:24.480
I'm going to compress these.

00:52:24.480 --> 00:52:27.990
So normally, you have some--

00:52:27.990 --> 00:52:29.690
how do you compute
your keys and values?

00:52:29.690 --> 00:52:33.030
You have your activations,
and you multiply them

00:52:33.030 --> 00:52:35.870
by some matrix to get K and
some other matrix to get V.

00:52:35.870 --> 00:52:40.230
And these are generally N times
H dimensions, like your model

00:52:40.230 --> 00:52:43.310
dim, which is big.

00:52:43.310 --> 00:52:46.910
So MLA says I'm going
to actually project

00:52:46.910 --> 00:52:50.510
these activations down
into C dimensions.

00:52:50.510 --> 00:52:55.050
So DeepSeek-V2 reduced
it from 16,000 to 512,

00:52:55.050 --> 00:52:57.630
so this is quite aggressive
compression here.

00:52:57.630 --> 00:53:00.830
And then I'm going to
compute the k and the V

00:53:00.830 --> 00:53:04.510
from this compressed
representation.

00:53:04.510 --> 00:53:07.990
So now, I can just store
C. This is much smaller.

00:53:07.990 --> 00:53:09.790
And then when I need
my keys and values,

00:53:09.790 --> 00:53:11.850
I can just materialize them.

00:53:14.390 --> 00:53:16.070
So there is one
wrinkle here, which

00:53:16.070 --> 00:53:19.070
is that MLA is not
compatible with rope,

00:53:19.070 --> 00:53:22.110
which operates directly
on the keys and values.

00:53:22.110 --> 00:53:25.540
So what they do is add
additional dimensions

00:53:25.540 --> 00:53:30.940
for handling the rope.

00:53:30.940 --> 00:53:36.100
But more or less, it's still
a pretty big reduction.

00:53:36.100 --> 00:53:39.180
So the latency and
throughput improvements

00:53:39.180 --> 00:53:42.200
follow by just simple math.

00:53:42.200 --> 00:53:47.940
The smaller the KV
cache, the faster you go.

00:53:47.940 --> 00:53:52.420
It's almost kind of linear
scaling up until at some point.

00:53:52.420 --> 00:53:54.020
And then, remember,
you need to check

00:53:54.020 --> 00:53:56.900
whether your model is accurate.

00:53:56.900 --> 00:54:00.460
So first of all,
this is the result

00:54:00.460 --> 00:54:07.060
that kind of contradicts or is
in tension with the GQA paper.

00:54:07.060 --> 00:54:11.040
They show that GQA
actually isn't that great.

00:54:17.680 --> 00:54:18.780
So this is MHA.

00:54:18.780 --> 00:54:19.640
This is GQA.

00:54:19.640 --> 00:54:23.140
These numbers are smaller
than these numbers.

00:54:23.140 --> 00:54:29.640
But they show that their method,
MLA, Multi-Latent Attention

00:54:29.640 --> 00:54:32.740
works even a little
bit better than MHA.

00:54:32.740 --> 00:54:35.820
But let's just say
it's about the same.

00:54:35.820 --> 00:54:39.360
So this column and this
column are much better than--

00:54:39.360 --> 00:54:41.580
I guess there's no
GQA on this table.

00:54:41.580 --> 00:54:43.740
You have to compare over here.

00:54:47.080 --> 00:54:50.600
So I'll show you an-- yeah.

00:54:50.600 --> 00:54:54.160
How does this compare
to reducing the number

00:54:54.160 --> 00:54:56.545
of dimensions, the model?

00:54:56.545 --> 00:54:57.920
So the question
is, how does this

00:54:57.920 --> 00:55:02.160
compare with reducing the
dimension of the model?

00:55:02.160 --> 00:55:03.880
So that's a good question.

00:55:03.880 --> 00:55:06.080
These ablations don't show that.

00:55:06.080 --> 00:55:08.280
My guess is that reducing
the model dimension

00:55:08.280 --> 00:55:12.920
just makes things worse
because you're indiscriminately

00:55:12.920 --> 00:55:15.440
just reducing everything.

00:55:15.440 --> 00:55:19.720
I think the trick in
all of this kind of

00:55:19.720 --> 00:55:23.550
is to find places in the
model where you can squeeze.

00:55:23.550 --> 00:55:25.710
And this apiary,
I don't think you

00:55:25.710 --> 00:55:27.250
can necessarily know for sure.

00:55:27.250 --> 00:55:29.167
You just have to do a
bunch of experimentation

00:55:29.167 --> 00:55:30.910
and see what works.

00:55:30.910 --> 00:55:34.470
So here's another idea
to reduce your KV cache.

00:55:34.470 --> 00:55:37.510
This is called
cross-layer attention.

00:55:37.510 --> 00:55:43.790
So the idea is that normally,
every layer has KV, K's and V's.

00:55:43.790 --> 00:55:47.790
But let's say instead of
doing that, we're just

00:55:47.790 --> 00:55:53.310
going to compute KVs for
a subset of the layers.

00:55:53.310 --> 00:55:56.590
And then, just for
this layer, I'm

00:55:56.590 --> 00:55:59.850
just going to use the
previous layers on KV cache.

00:56:05.150 --> 00:56:07.010
So this is another
way of sharing.

00:56:07.010 --> 00:56:11.030
Just like GQA shares
K across heads,

00:56:11.030 --> 00:56:15.030
now I'm sharing
KVs across layers.

00:56:15.030 --> 00:56:19.790
And empirically, this
paper shows that doing this

00:56:19.790 --> 00:56:22.000
improves the Pareto frontier.

00:56:22.000 --> 00:56:28.380
So each of these
models is better than--

00:56:28.380 --> 00:56:33.860
given a method you can always
sweep this the size of the KV

00:56:33.860 --> 00:56:41.060
cache by changing the K and the
head dimension, which, I guess,

00:56:41.060 --> 00:56:45.440
kind of relates to your point
about changing model dimension.

00:56:45.440 --> 00:56:53.700
But if you do this CLA cross
layer attention, it's better.

00:56:53.700 --> 00:56:59.180
So moving on, whirlwind
tour of different techniques

00:56:59.180 --> 00:57:01.280
for reducing KV cache.

00:57:01.280 --> 00:57:04.620
There's a local or
sliding window attention.

00:57:04.620 --> 00:57:06.900
This is a fairly
kind of old idea

00:57:06.900 --> 00:57:09.160
and a very kind of natural idea.

00:57:09.160 --> 00:57:12.780
So if you look at the
full attention matrix,

00:57:12.780 --> 00:57:14.260
it's N squared.

00:57:14.260 --> 00:57:16.820
And instead of doing
that, if you're

00:57:16.820 --> 00:57:22.010
going to generate a token, you
just look at the last K tokens.

00:57:22.010 --> 00:57:24.210
So you essentially
have a sliding window.

00:57:24.210 --> 00:57:29.610
For every token you generate,
you just depend on the last K.

00:57:29.610 --> 00:57:34.970
And now, if you do
that, the effective--

00:57:34.970 --> 00:57:37.450
so the KV cache is now
independent of the sequence

00:57:37.450 --> 00:57:37.950
length.

00:57:37.950 --> 00:57:46.170
It's just the number of the
batch times the other variables,

00:57:46.170 --> 00:57:47.190
which is great.

00:57:47.190 --> 00:57:51.330
And this is especially
great for long context.

00:57:51.330 --> 00:57:54.110
Now, because of the
number of layers,

00:57:54.110 --> 00:57:56.330
the effective context
length is actually

00:57:56.330 --> 00:57:59.150
larger than the number of
the stated context length

00:57:59.150 --> 00:58:03.570
because information can
propagate farther in

00:58:03.570 --> 00:58:05.890
if you go down the layers.

00:58:05.890 --> 00:58:07.290
Now, you can do fancier things.

00:58:07.290 --> 00:58:11.950
You can maybe not do a
dense selection of layers,

00:58:11.950 --> 00:58:13.750
but you can space it out.

00:58:13.750 --> 00:58:16.210
You can also do this
global plus sliding window

00:58:16.210 --> 00:58:20.760
where aware you have
attention to a fixed

00:58:20.760 --> 00:58:25.820
grid of different token points,
plus a local sliding window.

00:58:25.820 --> 00:58:27.760
So you can do various things.

00:58:27.760 --> 00:58:30.120
Now, the problem with
this is that it actually

00:58:30.120 --> 00:58:32.440
still hurts accuracy.

00:58:32.440 --> 00:58:34.420
So this reduces expressivity.

00:58:34.420 --> 00:58:38.520
There's no free lunch
here, or at least this

00:58:38.520 --> 00:58:41.600
was an expensive lunch.

00:58:41.600 --> 00:58:43.480
So the solution that
people come up with

00:58:43.480 --> 00:58:47.700
is that they interleave local
attention with global attention.

00:58:47.700 --> 00:58:52.880
So these hybrid models have full
attention for some of the layers

00:58:52.880 --> 00:58:57.800
and some local layers for
some of the other layers.

00:58:57.800 --> 00:58:59.560
And you're basically
always trying

00:58:59.560 --> 00:59:04.360
to-- and then so that allows
you to essentially reduce

00:59:04.360 --> 00:59:05.940
the KV cache a little bit.

00:59:05.940 --> 00:59:12.440
And you're always trying to
balance accuracy with the speed.

00:59:12.440 --> 00:59:13.340
Yeah.

00:59:13.340 --> 00:59:15.256
Hybrid model-wise,
is there a difference

00:59:15.256 --> 00:59:18.110
between the trade-off in
using a linear time invariant

00:59:18.110 --> 00:59:19.530
versus a sliding window?

00:59:19.530 --> 00:59:20.030
Yeah.

00:59:20.030 --> 00:59:23.790
So the question is, what about
linear attention versus sliding

00:59:23.790 --> 00:59:24.930
window attention?

00:59:24.930 --> 00:59:28.530
So I'm not going to talk
about linear attention.

00:59:28.530 --> 00:59:33.630
But very quickly, there's a
bunch of methods that where,

00:59:33.630 --> 00:59:40.270
instead of storing KV
cache, since you basically

00:59:40.270 --> 00:59:44.310
compute some of compressed
representation of all

00:59:44.310 --> 00:59:45.370
the history.

00:59:45.370 --> 00:59:47.790
So linear, the most
naive linear attention

00:59:47.790 --> 00:59:53.037
is you just sum the KV values
up into a single vector.

00:59:53.037 --> 00:59:55.370
So that's definitely independent
of the sequence length.

00:59:55.370 --> 00:59:56.490
You can do fancier things.

00:59:56.490 --> 00:59:58.950
There's gated net,
delta nets, and Mamba

00:59:58.950 --> 01:00:04.630
which allows you to try to
compress but not forget as much.

01:00:04.630 --> 01:00:07.010
Now the question is,
how do those compare?

01:00:07.010 --> 01:00:09.550
Those have also
been used in place

01:00:09.550 --> 01:00:14.220
of sliding window attention, and
people have gotten good results

01:00:14.220 --> 01:00:14.880
with them.

01:00:14.880 --> 01:00:17.480
You can also use a
combination of full attention,

01:00:17.480 --> 01:00:20.460
sliding window intention,
and the linear attention

01:00:20.460 --> 01:00:23.940
because they capture
different aspects.

01:00:23.940 --> 01:00:28.080
If you care about local
high-resolution stuff,

01:00:28.080 --> 01:00:30.540
then sliding attentions better.

01:00:30.540 --> 01:00:34.140
If you just want broad
summaries of the past,

01:00:34.140 --> 01:00:38.460
then the other linear
attention might be better.

01:00:38.460 --> 01:00:40.480
So then for a long
context sentence,

01:00:40.480 --> 01:00:42.220
would you say your
attention would

01:00:42.220 --> 01:00:45.860
be a better setting for that?

01:00:45.860 --> 01:00:48.620
So the question is, for long
context with linear attention

01:00:48.620 --> 01:00:50.240
be better?

01:00:54.140 --> 01:00:57.660
So there's no free lunch, right?

01:00:57.660 --> 01:00:59.740
Let's say you have
a very long context,

01:00:59.740 --> 01:01:03.100
and you're solving a needle
in a haystack problem.

01:01:03.100 --> 01:01:07.220
If you have to compress
your entire history

01:01:07.220 --> 01:01:12.000
into a small context, you're
just going to lose information,

01:01:12.000 --> 01:01:17.360
and you might just not
be able to retrieve it.

01:01:17.360 --> 01:01:19.922
I guess I feel like what
it seems like people

01:01:19.922 --> 01:01:21.880
go with hybrid architectures
that you'll always

01:01:21.880 --> 01:01:24.600
need some of longer
attention, but I'm just

01:01:24.600 --> 01:01:27.640
trying to understand in my head,
what the trade-off between using

01:01:27.640 --> 01:01:30.880
a sliding window versus some
of a Mamba, delta net layer

01:01:30.880 --> 01:01:31.800
would be.

01:01:31.800 --> 01:01:34.320
Is using a delta
net layer better

01:01:34.320 --> 01:01:36.120
than using sliding
window consistently?

01:01:36.120 --> 01:01:40.220
Or what is the actual maybe
representative trade-offs?

01:01:40.220 --> 01:01:41.280
Yeah.

01:01:41.280 --> 01:01:43.200
Maybe I'll say that
the Mamba and delta net

01:01:43.200 --> 01:01:46.805
are more powerful than
the sliding window.

01:01:46.805 --> 01:01:48.680
Attention maybe you can
think about the Mamba

01:01:48.680 --> 01:01:51.640
as probably can--

01:01:51.640 --> 01:01:54.520
it certainly can represent some
of the aspects of sliding window

01:01:54.520 --> 01:01:57.660
attention because you can just--
as you're doing the recurrence,

01:01:57.660 --> 01:01:59.860
it can just look
at the last state.

01:01:59.860 --> 01:02:06.360
So yeah, maybe you can think
about the linear attention

01:02:06.360 --> 01:02:10.710
or its extensions
as being better.

01:02:10.710 --> 01:02:12.230
They have more room.

01:02:12.230 --> 01:02:14.720
Once you do sliding window
attention, you're done.

01:02:14.720 --> 01:02:15.970
There's nothing else you can--

01:02:15.970 --> 01:02:16.470
[INAUDIBLE]

01:02:16.470 --> 01:02:17.630
Yeah.

01:02:17.630 --> 01:02:18.170
OK.

01:02:18.170 --> 01:02:20.790
So let me just quickly
go through this.

01:02:20.790 --> 01:02:23.390
Just highlight this DeepSeek.

01:02:23.390 --> 01:02:29.150
DeepSeek continues to innovate
different types of attention

01:02:29.150 --> 01:02:30.670
mechanisms.

01:02:30.670 --> 01:02:33.730
So remember, they came up with
the multi-latent attention,

01:02:33.730 --> 01:02:36.070
which compresses the key values.

01:02:36.070 --> 01:02:38.590
There is this thing called--

01:02:38.590 --> 01:02:41.205
now they have compressed
sparse attention, DeepSeek

01:02:41.205 --> 01:02:43.330
sparse attention, and
heavily compressed attention.

01:02:43.330 --> 01:02:48.558
I never remember all these
acronyms and what they mean,

01:02:48.558 --> 01:02:49.850
but let's look at this diagram.

01:02:49.850 --> 01:02:56.310
So normally, have your KV
tokens and your query token.

01:02:56.310 --> 01:03:01.110
So compressed attention is
going to basically compress

01:03:01.110 --> 01:03:04.950
every M tokens into one token.

01:03:04.950 --> 01:03:06.670
And then there's
this thing called

01:03:06.670 --> 01:03:11.260
DeepSeek sparse attention,
which basically selects

01:03:11.260 --> 01:03:15.300
a subset of those to keep.

01:03:15.300 --> 01:03:18.540
And the way you select a
subset is that you actually

01:03:18.540 --> 01:03:22.160
compute some LiDAR
weight queries and keys,

01:03:22.160 --> 01:03:24.340
and then you do a
smaller attention

01:03:24.340 --> 01:03:27.420
to get these index scores
so you know to keep.

01:03:27.420 --> 01:03:30.140
So a lightning fast
way to figure out

01:03:30.140 --> 01:03:32.000
what tokens you need to keep.

01:03:32.000 --> 01:03:34.500
And then you use those.

01:03:34.500 --> 01:03:40.060
And then, there's some more
compression that happens.

01:03:40.060 --> 01:03:44.140
So in the interest of
time, I'll move on.

01:03:44.140 --> 01:03:48.860
The goal of this section is to
reduce the KV cache because KV

01:03:48.860 --> 01:03:50.360
cache is related to memory.

01:03:50.360 --> 01:03:53.280
And we saw that inference
is memory-bound.

01:03:53.280 --> 01:03:57.020
So that directly
translates to improvements

01:03:57.020 --> 01:03:59.380
in throughput and latency.

01:03:59.380 --> 01:04:02.340
And the key is to do this
without hurting accuracy.

01:04:02.340 --> 01:04:07.650
So you can do lower dimensional
KV caches across layers

01:04:07.650 --> 01:04:13.010
by across heads and
across head dimension.

01:04:13.010 --> 01:04:14.870
You can do local attention.

01:04:14.870 --> 01:04:19.050
You can do linear attention,
which was discussed earlier,

01:04:19.050 --> 01:04:20.130
and there's much more.

01:04:20.130 --> 01:04:22.150
And there's also
diffusion models,

01:04:22.150 --> 01:04:24.690
which is a
non-autoregressive way

01:04:24.690 --> 01:04:28.330
to generate which
can be much faster.

01:04:28.330 --> 01:04:29.610
OK.

01:04:29.610 --> 01:04:33.270
So let's talk about a few other
ideas which are important.

01:04:33.270 --> 01:04:35.890
So quantization is
more of a, I would

01:04:35.890 --> 01:04:38.330
say, less of a
architecture and much more

01:04:38.330 --> 01:04:44.130
of a systems of perspective
on how to make things smaller.

01:04:44.130 --> 01:04:48.070
So the key idea here is just
reduce the precision of numbers.

01:04:48.070 --> 01:04:51.610
Less memory means higher
latency and throughput.

01:04:51.610 --> 01:04:54.770
And obviously, you have
to worry about accuracy.

01:04:54.770 --> 01:05:05.080
So quantization is-- there's
many options here all the way

01:05:05.080 --> 01:05:08.160
from bf16 all the
way down to int4.

01:05:11.240 --> 01:05:15.760
And so one thing you
can do, if you're

01:05:15.760 --> 01:05:19.060
scared that quantization
is going to mess you up,

01:05:19.060 --> 01:05:21.700
is that you train a model
with quantization in mind.

01:05:21.700 --> 01:05:24.920
So this is quantization
aware training.

01:05:24.920 --> 01:05:27.920
And during the feedforward
pass during training,

01:05:27.920 --> 01:05:31.080
you quantize and dequantize,
and you're basically

01:05:31.080 --> 01:05:35.000
simulating these quantization
errors as you train.

01:05:35.000 --> 01:05:37.520
So then, generally,
now the weights

01:05:37.520 --> 01:05:39.700
are adapted towards
quantization.

01:05:39.700 --> 01:05:40.860
Things will work better.

01:05:40.860 --> 01:05:45.600
But the con is that it requires
expensive large-scale training.

01:05:45.600 --> 01:05:49.200
And so what typically people
do is they train models,

01:05:49.200 --> 01:05:53.040
and then they quantize
after the fact.

01:05:53.040 --> 01:05:56.040
So this is post-training
quantization.

01:05:56.040 --> 01:05:59.920
It's much cheaper often.

01:05:59.920 --> 01:06:05.390
There's a naive way to do it is
that for every layer or tensor,

01:06:05.390 --> 01:06:10.310
you basically determine the
scale and the 0 point for each,

01:06:10.310 --> 01:06:11.390
let's say, tensor.

01:06:11.390 --> 01:06:15.750
And then you quantize
that separately.

01:06:15.750 --> 01:06:18.910
This generally
doesn't work as well.

01:06:18.910 --> 01:06:23.630
You can use this
idea called GPTQ,

01:06:23.630 --> 01:06:27.350
which uses some Hessian
information to quantize layer

01:06:27.350 --> 01:06:28.090
by layer.

01:06:28.090 --> 01:06:32.190
And then you keep track of the
errors which get propagated

01:06:32.190 --> 01:06:33.930
into the non-quantized weights.

01:06:33.930 --> 01:06:37.550
And so it allows you to
correct for the errors.

01:06:37.550 --> 01:06:40.150
And now, activation-aware
quantizing

01:06:40.150 --> 01:06:47.950
is more of a sophisticated
way where the observation is

01:06:47.950 --> 01:06:50.990
that some activation
channels are large

01:06:50.990 --> 01:06:54.630
and those the weights that
interact with those matter more.

01:06:54.630 --> 01:06:57.230
So let's allocate more
precision to these weights.

01:06:57.230 --> 01:06:59.710
So let's look at this picture.

01:06:59.710 --> 01:07:05.500
So normally, if you
take fp16 weight matrix,

01:07:05.500 --> 01:07:07.220
and you quantize it,
you get-- let's say

01:07:07.220 --> 01:07:09.460
you quantize it down to int3.

01:07:09.460 --> 01:07:13.700
But what you're going
to do is you figure out

01:07:13.700 --> 01:07:16.600
which of these are active?

01:07:16.600 --> 01:07:19.340
So these are maybe
activation channels.

01:07:19.340 --> 01:07:21.780
And some of these activation
channels are large.

01:07:21.780 --> 01:07:25.180
So if they're large
in general, then you

01:07:25.180 --> 01:07:30.060
basically allocate like, let's
say, fp16 for this channel.

01:07:30.060 --> 01:07:32.060
You keep everything
else as in three.

01:07:32.060 --> 01:07:33.920
And for a few
important channels,

01:07:33.920 --> 01:07:39.020
you use higher precision.

01:07:39.020 --> 01:07:45.220
So another idea here
is to do model pruning.

01:07:45.220 --> 01:07:47.780
So here you just
take a large model,

01:07:47.780 --> 01:07:51.080
and you rip out pieces
of it, and you fix it up.

01:07:51.080 --> 01:07:54.920
It's a very crude way,
but it turns out to work.

01:07:54.920 --> 01:08:00.760
So there's this paper from
NVIDIA where, essentially--

01:08:00.760 --> 01:08:05.300
let me actually-- let's see.

01:08:05.300 --> 01:08:08.680
So you first have to
estimate the importance

01:08:08.680 --> 01:08:11.440
of the different
parts of the model

01:08:11.440 --> 01:08:14.000
and choose the most
important parts.

01:08:14.000 --> 01:08:19.600
And then you basically
remove different hidden units

01:08:19.600 --> 01:08:24.319
and different even layers.

01:08:24.319 --> 01:08:26.840
And now, we have a model.

01:08:26.840 --> 01:08:29.479
It's not going to be very good.

01:08:29.479 --> 01:08:31.859
And so what you do
is you post-train it.

01:08:31.859 --> 01:08:34.640
You train it some more
on the data or the tasks

01:08:34.640 --> 01:08:37.960
that you care about to heal it.

01:08:37.960 --> 01:08:46.840
So this is, in some sense, a
training way to reduce the KV

01:08:46.840 --> 01:08:52.800
cache, but where you initialize
it with parts of a good model.

01:08:52.800 --> 01:08:55.819
So this seems to
work pretty well.

01:08:55.819 --> 01:08:59.830
So they were able to take a 15B
model and reduce it to an 8B

01:08:59.830 --> 01:09:01.510
model.

01:09:01.510 --> 01:09:06.390
And it doesn't really
hurt accuracy by too much.

01:09:06.390 --> 01:09:10.750
And the amount that you
use to train the model

01:09:10.750 --> 01:09:13.770
or through this
process is much less.

01:09:16.590 --> 01:09:17.090
OK.

01:09:17.090 --> 01:09:21.430
So just to summarize
here, the game

01:09:21.430 --> 01:09:25.029
is to reduce the
inference complexity

01:09:25.029 --> 01:09:26.830
without hurting accuracy.

01:09:26.830 --> 01:09:29.630
You can think about this as
mostly reducing the number

01:09:29.630 --> 01:09:31.910
of parameters or CPU cache.

01:09:31.910 --> 01:09:34.930
You can define a faster model
architecture to train it,

01:09:34.930 --> 01:09:39.090
or you can define a
faster model architecture.

01:09:39.090 --> 01:09:42.910
Initialize the weights
from original model, which

01:09:42.910 --> 01:09:44.510
might have a different
architecture,

01:09:44.510 --> 01:09:47.310
but you just make this
Frankenstein thing,

01:09:47.310 --> 01:09:53.109
and then you repair the faster
model with distillation.

01:09:53.109 --> 01:09:54.430
OK.

01:09:54.430 --> 01:09:54.930
Yeah.

01:09:54.930 --> 01:09:57.780
Could you say more about you
distinguish the important

01:09:57.780 --> 01:10:00.340
from the unimportant layers?

01:10:00.340 --> 01:10:02.860
So how do you distinguish
the important layers

01:10:02.860 --> 01:10:05.180
from the unimportant layers?

01:10:05.180 --> 01:10:10.740
So in general, you
have a calibration set,

01:10:10.740 --> 01:10:14.900
and you pass the inputs
through the model.

01:10:14.900 --> 01:10:18.220
And you're basically
looking at the magnitude

01:10:18.220 --> 01:10:20.180
of the activations.

01:10:20.180 --> 01:10:22.565
And the ones that
are-- some of them,

01:10:22.565 --> 01:10:23.940
especially if
they're dead units,

01:10:23.940 --> 01:10:25.600
will be kind of close to 0.

01:10:25.600 --> 01:10:28.580
And the ones that are
large, you want to keep.

01:10:28.580 --> 01:10:30.860
So that's a high-level idea.

01:10:30.860 --> 01:10:33.660
I guess why does it matter
if the activation is high?

01:10:33.660 --> 01:10:35.820
It could just be
like-- what if it's

01:10:35.820 --> 01:10:37.740
just always high, for instance?

01:10:37.740 --> 01:10:40.180
Or is that--

01:10:40.180 --> 01:10:43.920
So the question is, what if
all the activations are high?

01:10:43.920 --> 01:10:47.340
So in general, this is a kind
of an empirical observation

01:10:47.340 --> 01:10:52.060
that some of the channels will
be much higher than others.

01:10:52.060 --> 01:10:54.810
If this weren't true, then these
techniques wouldn't necessarily

01:10:54.810 --> 01:11:00.050
work, but it happens to
be true because that's

01:11:00.050 --> 01:11:03.650
how these models ended
up being trained.

01:11:03.650 --> 01:11:07.290
And then you can exploit that.

01:11:07.290 --> 01:11:09.250
Say a neuron, which
is always value

01:11:09.250 --> 01:11:11.810
100 across all the samples.

01:11:11.810 --> 01:11:14.750
Would that mean that it's
necessarily meaningful?

01:11:14.750 --> 01:11:16.810
Or maybe it's just an
artifact of training

01:11:16.810 --> 01:11:20.330
that I'm just like [INAUDIBLE]?

01:11:20.330 --> 01:11:20.950
Yeah, I see.

01:11:20.950 --> 01:11:24.850
So the question is, what
if a neuron is always 100?

01:11:24.850 --> 01:11:27.370
I mean, if that's the
case, you can also look

01:11:27.370 --> 01:11:30.970
at variance-related questions.

01:11:30.970 --> 01:11:32.650
If it's 100, you
can't just remove it

01:11:32.650 --> 01:11:34.930
because then everything
is going to be broken.

01:11:34.930 --> 01:11:36.990
If it's high mean
and low variance,

01:11:36.990 --> 01:11:41.050
maybe there's another a way
to just incorporate the bias

01:11:41.050 --> 01:11:43.230
essentially.

01:11:43.230 --> 01:11:43.730
OK.

01:11:43.730 --> 01:11:46.150
Let me quickly go
through this other idea.

01:11:46.150 --> 01:11:50.730
So far, we've looked at
lossy methods which basically

01:11:50.730 --> 01:11:54.640
really crunch down the KV cache,
but it could hurt accuracy.

01:11:54.640 --> 01:11:58.720
This is a very elegant way of
doing this in a lossless way.

01:11:58.720 --> 01:12:00.960
This is called speculative
sampling or speculative

01:12:00.960 --> 01:12:02.080
decoding.

01:12:02.080 --> 01:12:05.260
So remember that if
you're doing prefill,

01:12:05.260 --> 01:12:07.840
you can encode all the
tokens in parallel.

01:12:07.840 --> 01:12:09.380
This also gives
you probabilities.

01:12:09.380 --> 01:12:10.420
This is fast.

01:12:10.420 --> 01:12:13.320
This is compute-bound
and all nice things.

01:12:13.320 --> 01:12:16.280
And in generation,
it's one at a time.

01:12:16.280 --> 01:12:19.460
So checking is faster
than generation.

01:12:19.460 --> 01:12:22.520
If I give you a sequence,
it's fast to tell me

01:12:22.520 --> 01:12:26.320
how good it is, much faster than
it is to generate one at a time.

01:12:26.320 --> 01:12:28.720
So you can exploit
this asymmetry

01:12:28.720 --> 01:12:30.760
using the following idea.

01:12:30.760 --> 01:12:34.720
So what we're going to do
is use a cheap draft model

01:12:34.720 --> 01:12:38.780
to basically generate from
the guest a few tokens,

01:12:38.780 --> 01:12:40.098
let's say four tokens.

01:12:40.098 --> 01:12:42.640
And then we're going to use the
model we actually care about,

01:12:42.640 --> 01:12:47.440
the target model Q, which to
basically review these tokens

01:12:47.440 --> 01:12:50.200
and accept or not accept them.

01:12:50.200 --> 01:12:54.210
So things are kind of
chosen to be balanced.

01:12:54.210 --> 01:12:56.270
The draft model is just
smaller and cheaper.

01:12:56.270 --> 01:12:58.230
And so even though
it's memory-bound,

01:12:58.230 --> 01:13:01.150
and it has to generate one
at a time, is not too bad.

01:13:01.150 --> 01:13:04.010
Whereas, the target model
is big and expensive,

01:13:04.010 --> 01:13:05.670
but what we're
doing is asking it

01:13:05.670 --> 01:13:08.450
to process a batch
of tokens in parallel

01:13:08.450 --> 01:13:11.350
so it won't also be too bad.

01:13:11.350 --> 01:13:17.050
OK, so here's the video
that shows how things work.

01:13:17.050 --> 01:13:19.510
So if you use a big
model token by token,

01:13:19.510 --> 01:13:22.150
it's going to be pretty slow.

01:13:22.150 --> 01:13:27.670
But if you are doing
speculative decoding,

01:13:27.670 --> 01:13:35.150
then you can see the small model
generating a bunch of tokens,

01:13:35.150 --> 01:13:42.230
and then the large model
basically critiquing them.

01:13:42.230 --> 01:13:44.990
And then, so you basically
can get this burst

01:13:44.990 --> 01:13:47.790
of tokens and then maybe
another burst of tokens

01:13:47.790 --> 01:13:48.970
and so on and so forth.

01:13:51.820 --> 01:13:52.780
OK.

01:13:52.780 --> 01:13:56.740
So here is the
algorithm for-- there's

01:13:56.740 --> 01:13:59.660
a few papers that came out
with speculative decoding

01:13:59.660 --> 01:14:01.280
around the same time.

01:14:01.280 --> 01:14:04.380
This is one of them.

01:14:04.380 --> 01:14:09.220
And so the idea here is
that in order to generate,

01:14:09.220 --> 01:14:11.952
we're going to
generate K tokens.

01:14:11.952 --> 01:14:14.160
And we're just going to
sample from this draft model.

01:14:14.160 --> 01:14:16.560
So p is the draft model.

01:14:16.560 --> 01:14:18.500
We're going to sample K tokens.

01:14:18.500 --> 01:14:20.380
And then in
parallel, we're going

01:14:20.380 --> 01:14:27.220
to compute the logits of
these draft tokens using q.

01:14:27.220 --> 01:14:29.220
And then now we
have to determine

01:14:29.220 --> 01:14:31.040
whether we accept or not.

01:14:31.040 --> 01:14:34.900
And this is where you do a
bit of math and probability

01:14:34.900 --> 01:14:36.060
and statistics.

01:14:36.060 --> 01:14:41.420
You basically are going to
accept with probability Min 1

01:14:41.420 --> 01:14:45.260
over this ratio of q over p.

01:14:45.260 --> 01:14:52.630
So if q is much larger than
p, the larger the q is,

01:14:52.630 --> 01:14:54.390
the more likely you
want to accept it.

01:14:57.690 --> 01:15:03.290
And otherwise you sample from
this residual distribution

01:15:03.290 --> 01:15:04.770
and exit.

01:15:04.770 --> 01:15:09.730
So this is basically
rejection sampling

01:15:09.730 --> 01:15:11.310
except for rejection sampling.

01:15:11.310 --> 01:15:14.950
Sometimes when you
reject you get nothing.

01:15:14.950 --> 01:15:17.130
But here we always
are guaranteed

01:15:17.130 --> 01:15:20.610
to get an exact sample
from the target model.

01:15:20.610 --> 01:15:28.330
I'm going to skip
this simple proof.

01:15:28.330 --> 01:15:30.010
It's basically
the same arguments

01:15:30.010 --> 01:15:33.090
as rejection sampling
to show that it's

01:15:33.090 --> 01:15:40.850
the exact probabilities
from the target model.

01:15:40.850 --> 01:15:45.310
And the initial paper
shows that this fast.

01:15:45.310 --> 01:15:50.330
And generally, if you
have too few draft tokens,

01:15:50.330 --> 01:15:54.590
you're not really leveraging
the batching on the target model

01:15:54.590 --> 01:15:55.210
side.

01:15:55.210 --> 01:15:57.070
And if you have too
many, then you're

01:15:57.070 --> 01:15:58.650
going to reject more often.

01:15:58.650 --> 01:16:04.470
So there's a sweet spot around,
in this case, three or four.

01:16:04.470 --> 01:16:07.590
And in general, the draft
model is much smaller

01:16:07.590 --> 01:16:08.890
than the target model.

01:16:08.890 --> 01:16:11.670
And ideally, you want
the model draft model

01:16:11.670 --> 01:16:13.510
to be as close to
the target, which

01:16:13.510 --> 01:16:17.550
means that you
want to distill it,

01:16:17.550 --> 01:16:20.670
which means that actually a lot
of the same ideas that we just

01:16:20.670 --> 01:16:24.510
talked about are applicable to
speculative decoding as well.

01:16:24.510 --> 01:16:28.030
So basically, the
idea is that let's try

01:16:28.030 --> 01:16:32.290
to reduce your KV cache via
all the different shenanigans.

01:16:32.290 --> 01:16:35.030
And if you end up with a
model you're happy with,

01:16:35.030 --> 01:16:36.090
just serve that.

01:16:36.090 --> 01:16:37.910
If you're not
happy with it, then

01:16:37.910 --> 01:16:41.230
it at least can be a draft model
and you can use your main model

01:16:41.230 --> 01:16:43.390
to fix things up.

01:16:43.390 --> 01:16:45.580
There's a bunch of
whole literature

01:16:45.580 --> 01:16:48.100
on speculative
decoding right now

01:16:48.100 --> 01:16:55.780
that improve over the original,
which I'll skip for now.

01:16:55.780 --> 01:17:01.480
So very quickly now,
dynamic workloads.

01:17:01.480 --> 01:17:06.120
So this is the use case, is
you're serving a live website,

01:17:06.120 --> 01:17:09.060
and users come and
chat with your model.

01:17:09.060 --> 01:17:12.460
The requests arrive
at different times.

01:17:12.460 --> 01:17:14.600
They have different
shared prefixes,

01:17:14.600 --> 01:17:17.260
and they have different lengths.

01:17:17.260 --> 01:17:19.820
So it's kind of pretty messy.

01:17:19.820 --> 01:17:23.020
It's far from this very
simple training where

01:17:23.020 --> 01:17:27.900
you have these blocks of
the same number of tokens

01:17:27.900 --> 01:17:29.140
all at once.

01:17:29.140 --> 01:17:30.680
So what do you do in this case?

01:17:30.680 --> 01:17:35.020
So there's this idea--
there's a system called

01:17:35.020 --> 01:17:36.740
Orca that was built.
This is actually

01:17:36.740 --> 01:17:39.340
very early on which
introduced this idea

01:17:39.340 --> 01:17:41.340
of continuous batching.

01:17:41.340 --> 01:17:45.770
So the idea is that you
get a bunch of requests

01:17:45.770 --> 01:17:46.710
that look like this.

01:17:46.710 --> 01:17:49.290
So here's a prefix
of the first request,

01:17:49.290 --> 01:17:51.030
and you're generating
this token.

01:17:51.030 --> 01:17:52.448
Here's the second one.

01:17:52.448 --> 01:17:53.990
Here's the third
one, the fourth one.

01:17:53.990 --> 01:17:56.930
It's jagged because every
prefix has a different length.

01:17:56.930 --> 01:17:58.570
And what we're
going to do is we're

01:17:58.570 --> 01:18:00.930
going to decode step by step.

01:18:00.930 --> 01:18:05.710
So every step you decode one
token for all the sequences.

01:18:05.710 --> 01:18:08.510
Next step, you decode another
token for all the sequences.

01:18:08.510 --> 01:18:13.850
And then if you end, you
just eject that sequence.

01:18:13.850 --> 01:18:16.730
And then as new requests
arrive to the batch,

01:18:16.730 --> 01:18:19.730
then you basically
put it in the batch,

01:18:19.730 --> 01:18:22.913
and then you just continue.

01:18:22.913 --> 01:18:25.330
So that's why it's called
continuous batching because it's

01:18:25.330 --> 01:18:29.370
this batch is
dynamically being updated

01:18:29.370 --> 01:18:32.970
with either old finished
sequences being evicted

01:18:32.970 --> 01:18:34.770
and new ones coming in.

01:18:34.770 --> 01:18:39.250
So now, one problem
here is that everything

01:18:39.250 --> 01:18:41.760
we've seen batching works
when all the sequences have

01:18:41.760 --> 01:18:43.160
the same dimensionality.

01:18:43.160 --> 01:18:44.600
You have tensors.

01:18:44.600 --> 01:18:46.960
Every slice has the
same dimensionality.

01:18:46.960 --> 01:18:50.260
But if you request here
has a different length,

01:18:50.260 --> 01:18:51.840
so what do you do about that?

01:18:51.840 --> 01:18:54.200
So there's this idea
called selective batching

01:18:54.200 --> 01:19:00.640
where let's say you have a
length 3, length 9, and a length

01:19:00.640 --> 01:19:01.680
5.

01:19:01.680 --> 01:19:04.820
So here in the
attention computation,

01:19:04.820 --> 01:19:06.560
you can't really do
anything about this

01:19:06.560 --> 01:19:11.940
because attention depends on
the length of your sequence.

01:19:11.940 --> 01:19:16.140
So if you have a 3 by
3, a 9 by 9 computation,

01:19:16.140 --> 01:19:21.320
you can't really share
the tensor effectively.

01:19:21.320 --> 01:19:23.780
But for the non-attention,
the MLP layers,

01:19:23.780 --> 01:19:28.160
which takes up a lot of
FLOPs, you can actually just

01:19:28.160 --> 01:19:30.680
concatenate all the
sequences together

01:19:30.680 --> 01:19:34.720
to form a mega sequence,
and you process that.

01:19:34.720 --> 01:19:38.640
So final idea is page attention.

01:19:38.640 --> 01:19:41.910
This was introduced
in the BLM paper.

01:19:41.910 --> 01:19:44.250
Of course, BLM has many
other bells and whistles now,

01:19:44.250 --> 01:19:49.030
but this is the core
idea at that time.

01:19:49.030 --> 01:19:54.830
So the question is, how
is the KV cache stored?

01:19:54.830 --> 01:20:00.260
So if you think about
requests coming in,

01:20:00.260 --> 01:20:03.010
you have to put them
in memory somewhere.

01:20:03.010 --> 01:20:04.750
And in general,
there's this problem

01:20:04.750 --> 01:20:06.250
that you get fragmentation.

01:20:06.250 --> 01:20:08.070
This is what happens to--

01:20:08.070 --> 01:20:11.310
or used to happen
to your hard drive.

01:20:11.310 --> 01:20:15.630
And you have to defrag your
hard drive back in the day.

01:20:15.630 --> 01:20:19.730
So there's two types
of fragmentation.

01:20:19.730 --> 01:20:23.910
One is that you have to
allocate enough buffer so

01:20:23.910 --> 01:20:26.910
that-- because you don't
when you're going to stop.

01:20:26.910 --> 01:20:31.030
So you might have a max
token limit of 1,024.

01:20:31.030 --> 01:20:32.770
So you have to allocate
all this memory.

01:20:32.770 --> 01:20:34.228
And you can't put
anything in there

01:20:34.228 --> 01:20:36.150
because you're going
to just generate

01:20:36.150 --> 01:20:39.720
until you hit the max tokens,
and that's very wasteful.

01:20:39.720 --> 01:20:41.380
That's internal fragmentation.

01:20:41.380 --> 01:20:44.460
And then there's also you
have-- there could be space

01:20:44.460 --> 01:20:46.360
between different requests.

01:20:46.360 --> 01:20:50.040
And that space is maybe too
small to get used effectively.

01:20:50.040 --> 01:20:52.660
So that's just wasted space.

01:20:52.660 --> 01:20:56.738
So the solution here is just
these are systems people.

01:20:56.738 --> 01:20:58.280
So they know their
operating systems.

01:20:58.280 --> 01:21:01.380
They said, OK, well we've
solved this problem once before,

01:21:01.380 --> 01:21:04.180
so let's just use
the same idea here.

01:21:04.180 --> 01:21:06.780
So we're going to divide
the KV cache of a sequence

01:21:06.780 --> 01:21:11.440
into non-contiguous blocks, OK?

01:21:11.440 --> 01:21:14.780
So if you have this sequence,
four score and seven years

01:21:14.780 --> 01:21:16.500
ago our fathers
brought forth, we're

01:21:16.500 --> 01:21:19.460
just going to chunk it up
into these blocks of size 4.

01:21:19.460 --> 01:21:21.860
Doesn't matter where the
blocks go, but they're

01:21:21.860 --> 01:21:25.080
going to be aligned
according to the block.

01:21:25.080 --> 01:21:27.780
So there's some
uniformity there.

01:21:27.780 --> 01:21:32.980
So when two requests
share the same--

01:21:32.980 --> 01:21:35.080
can actually share
the same KV cache.

01:21:40.410 --> 01:21:45.130
So you might have this
block and this block.

01:21:45.130 --> 01:21:48.170
So this block might
go here and here,

01:21:48.170 --> 01:21:49.870
and this block
might be over there.

01:21:49.870 --> 01:21:52.710
So they're interspersed.

01:21:52.710 --> 01:21:54.310
But as long as you
have the indices

01:21:54.310 --> 01:21:58.410
and keep track of where
everything is, it's fine.

01:21:58.410 --> 01:22:05.330
So in particular, if you
have, system prompts, then

01:22:05.330 --> 01:22:09.890
you can actually just
cache these system prompts,

01:22:09.890 --> 01:22:12.330
the KV cache and the
system prompts, once.

01:22:12.330 --> 01:22:15.710
And that can be useful
for all the queries, OK?

01:22:15.710 --> 01:22:17.810
So this is very useful
because if a lot of people

01:22:17.810 --> 01:22:19.185
are using the same
system prompt,

01:22:19.185 --> 01:22:24.450
then you don't have to compute
the cache for every request.

01:22:24.450 --> 01:22:29.090
Also there are many applications
where you have the same prompt,

01:22:29.090 --> 01:22:31.650
and you actually want to
generate multiple responses.

01:22:31.650 --> 01:22:34.550
So in that case, you
can also just share

01:22:34.550 --> 01:22:39.070
the KB cache for
the prompt and just

01:22:39.070 --> 01:22:42.670
have unique
responses coming out.

01:22:42.670 --> 01:22:46.990
OK, So for example, if
you were to generate,

01:22:46.990 --> 01:22:50.150
let's say, multiple
generations from score

01:22:50.150 --> 01:22:55.550
and seven years ago are, blank,
and so what would happen here

01:22:55.550 --> 01:22:57.950
is that you would
have score and seven.

01:22:57.950 --> 01:23:02.750
And you would start by
having years ago or our.

01:23:02.750 --> 01:23:07.170
And then this is called
copy on write semantics.

01:23:07.170 --> 01:23:08.130
You keep this.

01:23:08.130 --> 01:23:10.290
And then we have
these two samples.

01:23:10.290 --> 01:23:14.510
And if they had happened to
sample let's say the same token,

01:23:14.510 --> 01:23:17.010
you just continue with that.

01:23:17.010 --> 01:23:19.670
But if they sample different
tokens, you split the block,

01:23:19.670 --> 01:23:22.030
and then you can continue there.

01:23:22.030 --> 01:23:25.110
So you're basically sharing
as much of the prefix cache

01:23:25.110 --> 01:23:28.310
as you can.

01:23:28.310 --> 01:23:33.060
There's a bunch of other
optimizations like kernels

01:23:33.060 --> 01:23:36.900
that I'm not going to
have time to go over.

01:23:36.900 --> 01:23:39.140
But the general
idea is that you're

01:23:39.140 --> 01:23:44.420
using these operating
systems metaphors to manage

01:23:44.420 --> 01:23:46.460
your inference.

01:23:46.460 --> 01:23:51.740
So summary here-- inference
is really, really important.

01:23:51.740 --> 01:23:54.220
It's very different
from training.

01:23:54.220 --> 01:23:57.060
Even though it's the same model,
but you're asking the model

01:23:57.060 --> 01:24:00.500
to do something very different,
ends up being very memory-bound.

01:24:00.500 --> 01:24:06.820
And it's also dynamic if you're
in a live chatbot use case.

01:24:06.820 --> 01:24:11.560
We saw a variety of different
techniques to improve inference.

01:24:11.560 --> 01:24:12.360
You can quantize.

01:24:12.360 --> 01:24:14.440
You can come up with
new architectures.

01:24:14.440 --> 01:24:16.280
You can prune and distill.

01:24:16.280 --> 01:24:19.380
You can also use
speculative sampling.

01:24:19.380 --> 01:24:22.380
But all of these are driven
by the same principle

01:24:22.380 --> 01:24:24.940
here, which is
reduce your KV cache

01:24:24.940 --> 01:24:28.540
but don't hurt
accuracy too much.

01:24:28.540 --> 01:24:33.730
And then there's ideas
from systems like paging

01:24:33.730 --> 01:24:35.850
and speculative
execution that can

01:24:35.850 --> 01:24:40.420
be brought to bear for actually
live inference servers.

01:24:40.420 --> 01:24:42.670
One thing we didn't really
get a chance to talk about,

01:24:42.670 --> 01:24:44.730
which was discussed
briefly, is that I

01:24:44.730 --> 01:24:48.210
think that new architectures
have actually a huge potential

01:24:48.210 --> 01:24:51.410
for improvement, things
like state space models

01:24:51.410 --> 01:24:54.810
or linear attention
or diffusion.

01:24:54.810 --> 01:24:58.330
At some level, the
KV cache and the way

01:24:58.330 --> 01:25:01.130
that attention is
built fundamentally

01:25:01.130 --> 01:25:06.010
makes it an inference
unfriendly kind of architecture.

01:25:06.010 --> 01:25:08.690
So if you can come up with
a new architecture that

01:25:08.690 --> 01:25:10.810
is designed for
inference in the way

01:25:10.810 --> 01:25:15.330
that the transformer was not,
this can maybe unlock a lot.

01:25:15.330 --> 01:25:18.090
OK, so I will stop there.

01:25:18.090 --> 01:25:21.210
And next class, Tatsu
will return and talk

01:25:21.210 --> 01:25:24.280
about scaling laws part 2.

